a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and\n     \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).\n\n     *If \\(b\\le n-k\\)*, then \\(b = j-(k+1)\\) for some \\(j\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\);\n     consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_j\\).\n\n     *If \\(b> n-k\\)*, write \\(b = i\\) with \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n+1-i\\le k+1\\).  Substituting\n     \\(a\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) gives  \n\n     \\[\n     y = \\frac{i}{k+1}a + \\frac{k+1-i}{k+1}\n       = \\frac{ia + k+1-i}{k+1}\n       = \\frac{i(a-1)+k+1}{k+1}=i,\n     \\]\n\n     so \\((a,b)=(a,i)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\n     Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) belongs to at least one of the\n     constructed lines.\n\n   - The family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\))\n     and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny horizontal lines, all distinct.  Thus the required\n     configuration exists for the chosen \\(k\\).\n\n3. Combining the necessity and sufficiency parts yields the complete\n   description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) contains exactly the lattice points of the right\u2011\nisosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); it has \\(n\\) distinct\n\\(x\\)-coordinates \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) and also \\(n\\) distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\nFix an \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(x_0\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nIf a sunny line \\(L\\) contains two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) with the same\n\\(x\\)-coordinate \\(x_0\\), then those two points would have the same\n\\(y\\)-coordinate, forcing \\(L\\) to be horizontal, contradicting the\ndefinition of sunny.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one**\npoint of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate equals a prescribed value.\n\nSince the \\(x\\)-coordinates \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) are all distinct, at most one\nsunny line can be assigned to each of them.  Consequently any family of\n\\(n\\) lines covering \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) in the indicated range.  We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together cover \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  The sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x + \\frac{k+1-i}{k+1}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{k+1}\\) is a positive rational number different from\n\\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); the line is not vertical either.  Hence each \\(L_i\\) is\nsunny.  Substituting \\(x=k+1\\) into (1) gives \\(y=i\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n(k+1,i)\\in L_i\\cap\\mathcal{P}_n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus each sunny line passes through a distinct point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The non\u2011sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(k+1\\le j\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y = j-(k+1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_j\\) is a horizontal line, hence non\u2011sunny.  The lines\n\\(H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_n\\) are distinct because their constant terms are\ndifferent.\n\n#### 2.3  Verification that all points are covered\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\).  Two cases occur.\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\le n-k\\).*  \nThen \\(b = j-(k+1)\\) for the unique integer \\(j = b+k+1\\) belonging to\n\\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  By definition (3) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the\nhorizontal line \\(H_j\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b> n-k\\).*  \nSince \\(b\\le n\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); write \\(b=i\\).  From\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-i\\le k+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) with this \\(i\\) we compute  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{k+1}a+\\frac{k+1-i}{k+1}\n   =\\frac{ia+k+1-i}{k+1}\n   =\\frac{i(a-1)+k+1}{k+1}=i=b .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\) and therefore belongs to\n\\(L_i\\).\n\nIn both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) is contained in at least one of the\nconstructed lines.  Consequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers the whole set \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\).\n\n#### 2.4  Counting sunny lines\n\nExactly the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny, so the number of sunny\nlines in the family equals the prescribed \\(k\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a\nconfiguration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of\nthe problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\) together with the explicit construction\nfor each admissible \\(k\\) shows that the set of all possible values of\n\\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nNo other values can occur, and every value in this interval is\nrealizable. \u220e"
