:* \\(b> n-k\\).  \n     Write \\(b=i\\) with \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have\n     \\(a\\le n+1-i\\le k+1\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (1) gives  \n\n     \\[\n     y=\\frac{i}{k+1}a+\\frac{k+1-i}{k+1}\n       =\\frac{ia+k+1-i}{k+1}\n       =i=b .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\n     In either case the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by at least one of the\n     constructed lines.\n\n   - The family consists of exactly the \\(k\\) sunny lines\n     \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) and the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines\n     \\(H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_n\\); all are distinct.  Thus a configuration with\n     precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n3. Combining the upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) with the explicit construction for\n   every \\(k\\) in \\([0,n]\\) yields the complete description of admissible\n   values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) consists of the integer lattice points inside the\nright‑isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); it contains\nexactly \\(n\\) distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) and also \\(n\\)\ndistinct \\(y\\)-coordinates.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).  Fix an \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(x_0\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  If a sunny line\ncontained two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with that same \\(x\\)-coordinate,\nthose two points would have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, forcing the line\nto be horizontal, contradicting the definition of sunny.  Hence a sunny\nline can contain **at most one** point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose\n\\(x\\)-coordinate equals a prescribed value.\n\nSince the \\(x\\)-coordinates \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) are all distinct, at most one\nsunny line can be assigned to each of them.  Therefore any family of\n\\(n\\) lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny\nlines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  Sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x+\\frac{k+1-i}{k+1}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{k+1}\\) is a positive rational number different from\n\\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\nEvaluating (4) at \\(x=k+1\\) gives \\(y=i\\); thus  \n\n\\[\n(k+1,i)\\in L_i\\cap\\mathcal P_n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(k\\) sunny lines are distinct and each passes through a\ndifferent point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Non‑sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j-(k+1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_j\\) is a horizontal line, therefore non‑sunny, and the lines\n\\(H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_n\\) are distinct because their constant terms are\ndifferent.\n\n#### 2.3  Verification that all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are covered\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  Two mutually exclusive\nsituations occur.\n\n*Case 1.* \\(b\\le n-k\\).  \nThen \\(b=j-(k+1)\\) for the unique integer\n\\(j=b+k+1\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  By definition (6) the point\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_j\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(b> n-k\\).  \nSince \\(b\\le n\\), we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); write \\(b=i\\).\nFrom the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-i\\le k+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) (4) yields  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{i}{k+1}a+\\frac{k+1-i}{k+1}\n   =\\frac{ia+k+1-i}{k+1}\n   =i=b .\n\