_{i=1}^{n}\\nu(L_i)\\le k\\,(n-1)+(n-k)\\,n=n^2-n+k. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(S\\) must belong to at least one line, so the left–hand side of (6)\nis at least \\(|S|\\).  From (3) and (6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\le n^2-n+k\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\ge n-2.\n\\]\n\nHence **at most** \\(n-2\\) of the lines can be sunny:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{U}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Two non‑sunny lines*  \n\n\\[\nL_{v}:\\;x=1 ,\\qquad L_{h}:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are forbidden directions, therefore non‑sunny.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + (n+2-2i). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of each \\(L_i\\) equals \\(1\\); this is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so every\n\\(L_i\\) is sunny.  The intercepts are distinct, hence the lines are distinct.\nEquation (7) shows that \\(L_i\\) passes through the lattice point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+2-i)\\in S. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nMoving one unit to the right from \\(P_i\\) we obtain the point \\((i+1,n+1-i)\\in S\\);\ncontinuing, the whole set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i+t,\\;n+2-i-t)\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,n-i\\}\n\\]\n\nlies on \\(L_i\\).  Thus \\(L_i\\) covers **all** points of column \\(i\\) that belong to\n\\(S\\).\n\n*Additional non‑sunny lines (if needed).*  \nIf \\(k<n-2\\) we need \\(n-2-k\\) more lines.  \nFor each integer \\(j=2,\\dots ,n-k-1\\) take the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non‑sunny and are all distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Verification that the construction covers \\(S\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{v}\\).\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{h}\\).\n* Assume \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).\n\n  *If \\(a\\le k\\).*  \n    By (8) the point \\((a,n+2-a)\\) belongs to \\(L_a\\).  \n    The points of column \\(a\\) that belong to \\(S\\) are exactly  \n\n    \\[\n    (a,1),(a,2),\\dots ,(a,n+2-a).\n    \\]\n\n    Starting from \\((a,n+2-a)\\) and moving downward one unit at a time stays on the\n    same vertical line \\(x=a\\); each step reduces the \\(y\\)‑coordinate by \\(1\\) while\n    keeping the point on \\(L_a\\) because the equation of \\(L_a\\) can be rewritten as\n    \\(y = (n+2-a) - (x-a)\\).  Hence every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+2-a\\)\n    lies on \\(L_a\\).  In particular our given \\((a,b)\\) is on \\(L_a\\).\n\n  *If \\(a>k\\).*  \n    From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-k-1\\).  \n    Thus \\(b\\) is an integer with \\(2\\le b\\le n-k-1\\); the horizontal line \\(H_b\\)\n    (present because \\(k<n-2\\) or, when \\(k=n-2\\), the case \\(a>k\\) cannot occur) contains\n    \\((a,b)\\).\n\nAll points of \\(S\\) are therefore covered.  The family consists of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny, and the remaining\n\\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary condition (U) together with the explicit construction shows that the\nset of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
