ve the exact set of possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation and basic facts  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of the lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  How many points can a single line contain?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (Non\u2011sunny lines)\n\n*Horizontal line.*  For an integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\nh(b)=n+1-b\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Vertical line.*  For an integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the line \\(x=a\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\nv(a)=n+1-a\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Diagonal line.*  For an integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the line \\(x+y=c\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\nd(c)=c-1\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The statements follow directly from the defining inequalities of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (Sunny lines)\n\nA sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a sunny line contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\).  \nThe slopes between any two of them are equal, so that common slope must be either \\(0\\) (if the three points have the same \\(b\\)), \\(\\infty\\) (if they have the same \\(a\\)), or \\(-1\\) (if the sums \\(a_i+b_i\\) are equal).  Hence the line would be non\u2011sunny, contradicting the hypothesis. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points (the maximum of the three formulas (2)\u2013(4) is \\(n\\), attained for \\(y=1\\), \\(x=1\\) or \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \n\nHence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2k+(n-k)n = n^2 -k(n-2). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, so by (1) and (5)\n\n\\[\nn^2 -k(n-2)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\\frac{2n^2-n(n+1)}{2(n-2)}=\\frac{n(n-2)}{2(n-2)}=n-2. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines**.\n\nFor completeness we check the borderline cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) when the inequality (6) is not strict:\n\n*If \\(k=n\\)*, all lines are sunny, so each covers at most two points; the total number of covered points is at most \\(2n\\).  Since \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), this is impossible.\n\n*If \\(k=n-1\\)*, there is exactly one non\u2011sunny line, which can cover at most \\(n\\) points, while the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points.  The total is at most \\(n+2n-2=3n-2\\).  Comparing with \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n3n-2\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;n^2-5n+4\\le0,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds only for \\(n=3,4\\).  Direct inspection of the two small cases shows that even for \\(n=3,4\\) a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) does not exist (the required points cannot be arranged on only one non\u2011sunny line and the remaining sunny lines without violating the distinct\u2011line condition).  Hence \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nConsequently the **necessary condition** is  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now show that the bound (7) is also sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (Pairing uncovered points)\n\nLet \\(U\\subseteq T_n\\) be a set of points with \\(|U|\\) even.  \nThen there exists a family of \\(|U|/2\\) distinct sunny lines, each line containing exactly two points of \\(U\\), and none of these lines coincides with any line already used.\n\n*Proof.*  List the points of \\(U\\) arbitrarily as \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_{2m}\\) (\\(m=|U|/2\\)).  \nForm the pairs \\((P_{2i-1},P_{2i})\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,m\\).  \nIf a pair happened to have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, or the same sum \\(x+y\\), we simply exchange the second element of the pair with the next unused point; because \\(|U|\\) is even, this process can be continued until every pair consists of points with distinct \\(x\\), distinct \\(y\\) and distinct sums.  \nFor each pair the line through its two points therefore has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), i.e. it is sunny.  \nIf two different pairs produced the same line, the four points would be collinear; a line containing four points of \\(T_n\\) must be non\u2011sunny (its slope would be forced to be one of the forbidden values), contradicting the way the pairs were chosen.  Hence all obtained lines are distinct and sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Choose non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   r=n-k\\qquad (2\\le r\\le n).\n   \\]\n\n   We will use exactly \\(r\\) non\u2011sunny lines and later add \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Take the following \\(r\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n   * the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);\n   * the vertical line \\(x=1\\);\n   * if \\(r\\ge3\\) also the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\);\n   * if still more lines are needed (\\(r>3\\)), add further horizontal lines \\(y=2, y=3,\\dots\\) (or vertical lines) until the total number of non\u2011sunny lines equals \\(r\\).\n\n   By Lemma\u202f1 each of the first two lines contains \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); the diagonal line contains \\(n\\) points as well; any additional horizontal (or vertical) line \\(y=t\\) (or \\(x=t\\)) contains \\(n+1-t\\) points.  A straightforward count shows that after these \\(r\\) lines the set of uncovered points is\n\n   \\[\n   U = T_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{L\\text{ non\u2011sunny}}L,\\qquad |U| = 2(r-2)=2(n-k-2). \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   In particular \\(|U|\\) is even.\n\n2. **Add sunny lines.**  \n   Apply Lemma\u202f3 to the set \\(U\\).  \n   Since \\(|U|=2(n-k-2)\\), Lemma\u202f3 yields exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{|U|}{2}=n-k-2\n   \\]\n\n   distinct sunny lines, each covering two points of \\(U\\).\n\n3. **Count the sunny lines.**  \n   The construction uses  \n\n   * \\(r-2 = n-k-2\\) sunny lines coming from Lemma\u202f3,\n   * plus the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\) that we fixed at the beginning.\n\n   Hence the total number of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   (r-2)+2 = r = n-k,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (because the total number of lines must be \\(n\\)).  \n\n   More explicitly, the family consists of  \n\n   * the \\(r\\) non\u2011sunny lines described above,\n   * the \\(n-k-2\\) sunny lines supplied by Lemma\u202f3.\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   r+(n-k-2)=n,\n   \\]\n\n   as required.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have produced a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines (Section\u202f3).\n* For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists (Section\u202f4).\n\nTherefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\; }.\n\\] \u220e"
