obtain  \n\n\\[\n-(n-1)\\le b-a\\le 0,\n\\]\n\nso indeed \\(c\\) belongs to the index set of (6).  The equality \\(b=a+c\\) shows that \\((a,b)\\in L_c\\).  Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines (6) cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Define three families of lines.\n\n* **Sunny lines** (slope \\(1\\)):\n  \\[\n  S_c:\\; y = x + c ,\\qquad c = -(k-1),-(k-2),\\dots ,0 .\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n  There are exactly \\(k\\) such lines, all sunny.\n\n* **Vertical non‑sunny lines**:\n  \\[\n  V_j:\\; x=j ,\\qquad j = k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n .\n  \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n\n* **Horizontal non‑sunny lines**:\n  \\[\n  H_j:\\; y=j ,\\qquad j = 1,2,\\dots ,n-k .\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\); the only sunny ones are those in (7).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Verification of the covering property\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nd:=b-a .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the integer \\(d\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n-(n-1)\\le d\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nWe distinguish three cases.\n\n* **Case 1: \\(-k+1\\le d\\le 0\\).**  \n  Then \\(c:=d\\) belongs to the index set of (7); the equality \\(b=a+c\\) shows \\((a,b)\\in S_c\\).  Thus the point lies on a sunny line.\n\n* **Case 2: \\(d\\ge 1\\).**  \n  Here \\(b>a\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n  \\[\n  b-1\\le n-a\\le n-k .\n  \\]\n\n  Hence \\(b-1\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n-k\\); the horizontal line \\(H_{\\,b-1}\\) defined in (9) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n* **Case 3: \\(d\\le -k\\).**  \n  Then \\(a>b\\) and \\(a\\ge k+1\\).  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(a\\le n\\).  Consequently the vertical line \\(V_a\\) from (8) exists and passes through \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThe three cases cover all possibilities for \\(d\\); therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (7)–(9).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), an explicit set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTogether with the obvious bound (5), this proves that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
