(-1\\).  \n   Hence a non\u2011sunny line can contain **at most one** of these points.  \n   Since there are \\(n\\) such points but only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, at least one of the points would have to lie on the unique non\u2011sunny line, forcing that line to have slope \\(-1\\).  \n   But a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most one point of the set, contradicting the requirement that all \\(n\\) points be covered.  \n   Therefore a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\nThese three statements constitute the rigorous partial solution obtained.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\; x=i .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(V_i\\) are non\u2011sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(V_a\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\) cover every point of \\(T_n\\) and contain no sunny line.  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}\\neq 0,\\qquad\n\\frac{i}{i+1}\\neq -1,\\qquad\n\\frac{i}{i+1}\\neq \\infty ,\n\\]\n\nso every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) we may substitute \\(i=a\\) in (1) and obtain  \n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{a}{a+1}\\,a + \\frac{1}{a+1}\n   = \\frac{a^2 + 1}{a+1}\n   = \\frac{a(a+1) - a + 1}{a+1}\n   = a - \\frac{a-1}{a+1}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\); a direct verification shows that the right\u2011hand side of (1) with \\(i=a\\) equals exactly this integer \\(b\\).  Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \n\nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\), giving a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non\u2011existence of a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nLet \\(M\\) be the unique non\u2011sunny line in \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).  By definition, \\(M\\) is either\n\n* horizontal (\\(y=c\\)),\n* vertical (\\(x=c\\)), or\n* of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(1,n), (2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery element of \\(S_n\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\) because each satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \nFor any two distinct points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) and \\((a',n+1-a')\\) in \\(S_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1-a')-(n+1-a)}{a'-a}= \\frac{a-a'}{a'-a}= -1,\n\\]\n\nso the line through any pair of points of \\(S_n\\) has slope \\(-1\\).\n\nConsequences:\n\n* A horizontal line can contain at most one point of \\(S_n\\) (different \\(y\\)-coordinates).\n* A vertical line can contain at most one point of \\(S_n\\) (different \\(x\\)-coordinates).\n* A line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most **one** point of \\(S_n\\) as well, because any two points of \\(S_n\\) already determine the unique line of slope \\(-1\\) passing through them, and that line would then contain the whole set, contradicting distinctness of the points.\n\nHence **any** non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one element of \\(S_n\\).\n\nSince \\(|S_n|=n\\) and there are only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, at least one point of \\(S_n\\) would have to lie on the non\u2011sunny line \\(M\\).  But \\(M\\) can contain at most one such point, leaving at least one other point of \\(S_n\\) uncovered\u2014a contradiction to the covering requirement.\n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* \\(k=0\\) is realizable (vertical\u2011line construction).\n* \\(k=n\\) is realizable (the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^n\\) defined in (1)).\n* \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible (argument using the set \\(S_n\\)).\n\nThe status of the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) remains unresolved by the present work.  Determining precisely which of these integers can occur is left as an open problem."
