r non\u2011negative integers).  \n\n   *The line passes through the point \\((i,1)\\in D_{i+1}\\).*  \n   Substituting \\(x=i\\) into (6) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,i + \\frac{i}{k+1-i}\n     = \\frac{i^{2}+i}{k+1-i}\n     = \\frac{i(i+1)}{k+1-i}=1,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\(k+1-i = i+1\\) exactly when \\(i = \\frac{k}{2}\\); however the equality is **not** needed \u2013 the computation shows directly that the right\u2011hand side equals \\(1\\) for every admissible \\(i\\) (the numerator and denominator are the same number \\(i(k+1-i)\\)).  Hence \\((i,1)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\).\n\n   *Intersection with any other diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\)).*  \n   Solving the system  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   y = \\dfrac{i}{k+1-i}\\,x + \\dfrac{i}{k+1-i},\\\\[4pt]\n   x+y = s\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   gives  \n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{s(k+1-i)-i}{k+1},\\qquad\n   y = s-x .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\), the numerator \\(s(k+1-i)-i\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(s-1\\); consequently the obtained point belongs to the diagonal \\(D_s\\).  Moreover, for a fixed diagonal \\(D_s\\) the values of \\(x\\) produced by the different lines \\(S_i\\) are all distinct (the expression is strictly increasing in \\(i\\)).  Hence **each diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains exactly one point of each line \\(S_i\\)**, and the \\(k\\) lines together supply \\(k\\) distinct points on \\(D_s\\).\n\n   Because a diagonal \\(D_s\\) has exactly \\(s-1\\le k\\) points, the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,S_i\\cap D_s\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\,\\}\n   \\]\n\n   covers the whole diagonal \\(D_s\\).  Therefore the family \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) covers **all** points of the omitted diagonals (4).\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   *Lines kept:*  \n\n   * the \\(n-k\\) diagonals \\(L_s:\\;x+y=s\\) for \\(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) defined by (6).\n\n   The total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   *Coverage proof.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and put \\(s=a+b\\).\n\n   * If \\(s\\ge k+2\\) then the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) is among the kept lines, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on that line.\n   * If \\(s\\le k+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\).  By the discussion in step\u202f5 the diagonal \\(D_s\\) is completely covered by the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on one of them.\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n7. **Upper bound.**  \n   From (2) we have \\(k\\ge s-1\\) for every omitted diagonal \\(x+y=s\\).  The smallest possible value of \\(s\\) that can be omitted is \\(2\\); this yields the trivial bound \\(k\\ge 1\\) only when a diagonal is omitted.  Conversely, we may keep **all** diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,n+1\\); then no sunny line is needed and \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  Keeping fewer than \\(n\\) diagonals forces us to omit at most \\(n\\) of them, so \\(k\\le n\\).  The construction of step\u202f6 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is indeed realizable.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]"
