of.*  \nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \n\n* Horizontal:  \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points lie on the line.  \n\n* Vertical:  analogous.  \n\n* Diagonal:  \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  The admissible points are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\) of them. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (At least two non‑sunny lines)\n\nIn any family \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(\\mathcal{P}\\), the number \\(t\\) of non‑sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge 2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(t\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines and \\(s=n-t\\) the number of sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 each non‑sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\); a sunny line also covers at most \\(n\\) points (any line meets the integer lattice in at most \\(n\\) points of the triangle, because the triangle has side length \\(n\\)).  Hence the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n\\[\ntn+sn=n\\,t+n\\,s=n\\,n .\n\\]\n\nSince the family must cover all \\(|\\mathcal{P}|=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\\) points, we need  \n\n\\[\ntn+sn\\ge\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n\\,t\\ge\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}-n\\,s .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s=n-t\\), this simplifies to  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}-\\frac{n-t}{2}= \\frac{t+1}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich yields \\(t\\ge 2\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Corollary 3 (Upper bound for sunny lines)\n\nFrom Lemma 2 we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk=n-t\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration contains at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction attaining the bound\n\nDefine two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{h}: