ily \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)\n   is covered either by the vertical line \\(x=a\\) or by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n   After the replacement of \\(x=i\\) by \\(S_i\\) we check the two possible kinds of points\n   that could have lost their original line:\n\n   * Points \\((i,1)\\): they remain on the new line \\(S_i\\).\n   * Points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\): they are still covered by the horizontal line\n     \\(y=b\\), which has not been altered.\n\n   Hence the covering property is unchanged.\n\n4. **Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   Choose any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).  \n   Starting from the non\u2011sunny covering \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) (which contains the vertical\n   lines \\(x=i\\) for all \\(i\\) and the horizontal lines \\(y=j\\) for all\n   \\(j\\neq n\\)), replace each vertical line \\(x=i\\) with \\(i\\in I\\) by the sunny line\n   \\(S_i\\) defined in (1).  \n\n   The resulting family \\(\\mathcal C_{k}\\) consists of  \n\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\);\n   * the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(\\{x=i\\mid i\\notin I\\}\\);\n   * the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\).\n\n   Since we have used \\(2n-1\\) lines, we discard any \\(n-1\\) of the horizontal lines\n   (for instance the largest ones).  After discarding, exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines\n   remain, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.  \n\n   By the lemma, the covering of all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is still\n   valid, because the only points that could have lost a vertical line are still\n   covered by the unchanged horizontal lines.\n\n5. **All values are attainable.**  \n   * \\(k=0\\): keep the original non\u2011sunny family \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\).  \n   * \\(1\\le k\\le n\\): perform the replacement described above for any \\(k\\) indices.\n   In every case we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines, the covering condition holds, and\n   exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n6. **Conclusion** \u2013 the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nWe must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, each of which is either sunny or non\u2011sunny.\nHence\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A covering with only non\u2011sunny lines\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{0}= \\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\cup\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) are vertical or horizontal, therefore non\u2011sunny.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le n-1\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) and contains\n\\((a,b)\\).  \nIf \\(b=n\\) then \\(a\\le 1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), so \\(a=1\\) and the vertical line\n\\(x=1\\) (which is in \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\)) contains \\((1,n)\\).  \nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\).\nConsequently \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Sunny replacement lemma\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x-i+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover \\(S_i\\) passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((i+1,2)\\).\n\n*Lemma.* Replacing the vertical line \\(x=i\\) in \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) by the sunny line\n\\(S_i\\) leaves the covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) intact.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe only points that could lose the line \\(x=i\\) are those with abscissa \\(i\\).\n* The point \\((i,1)\\) remains on the new line \\(S_i\\) because \\((i,1)\\) satisfies (5).\n* Any point \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) is still covered by the horizontal line\n\\(y=b\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\) and is unchanged. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and choose a subset  \n\n\\[\nI\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad |I|=k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal C_{0}\\), replace each vertical line \\(x=i\\) with \\(i\\in I\\)\nby the sunny line \\(S_i\\) given by (5).  \nDenote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal C_{k}\\).  \nBy the lemma, \\(\\mathcal C_{k}\\) still covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal C_{k}\\) now contains  \n\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\);\n* the \\(n-k\\) unchanged vertical lines \\(\\{x=i\\mid i\\notin I\\}\\);\n* the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal C_{k}\\) consists of \\(2n-1\\) distinct lines.  \nTo obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines we delete any \\(n-1\\) of the horizontal lines\n(e.g. the lines \\(y=n-1, y=n-2,\\dots ,y=n-(n-1)\\)).  \nDeletion does not affect the covering because every point \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(b\\ge 1\\) is already covered either by a vertical line (if \\(b\\) was removed) or\nby a sunny line (if the point is \\((i,1)\\) for some \\(i\\in I\\)).  \n\nAfter the deletion we have precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly the\n\\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.  Hence a configuration with any prescribed\n\\(k\\) (subject to (3)) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nWe have 