y cover every point of \\(S_{n}\\). This is correct.\n\n#### 6.2 Case \\(k=n\\)  \nThe solution claims:\n\n> \u201cFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(1\\le b-a\\le n\\) and, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) have opposite parity; therefore \\(b-a\\) is odd, say \\(b-a=2i-1\\), and the point lies on \\(L_{i}\\).\u201d\n\nThe statement \u201c\\(a\\) and \\(b\\) have opposite parity\u201d is **false**.  \nExample: \\(n=4\\), point \\((2,2)\\) belongs to \\(S_{4}\\) because \\(2+2=4\\le5\\); both coordinates are even. Hence \\(b-a=0\\) (even), and the point is not on any line \\(y=x+(2i-1)\\) (all such lines have odd intercept). Thus the covering fails for this edge case as well.\n\n*Therefore the argument for \\(k=n\\) is also incorrect.*\n\n### 7.  Necessity of the Bound (Section\u202f7)  \nThe observation that at most \\(n\\) lines can be sunny because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total is trivial and correct. However, it does not address the possibility that some values \\(k\\le n\\) might be impossible; the earlier flawed construction was intended to show they are all possible, but that proof is invalid.\n\n### 8.  Conclusion (Section\u202f8)  \nThe final statement that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable relies on the erroneous arguments in Sections\u202f3 and\u202f6. Consequently the conclusion is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution contains critical logical and algebraic errors that invalidate the covering argument for many admissible values of \\(k\\). Therefore the answer \u201call \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\u201d is not proven, and the solution is **incorrect**."

