e cannot contain any point of \\(S_n\\) because all points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\ge 2\\). Hence at most one point of each \\(D_c\\) can lie on a given line. \u220e\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, i.e. \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\).  \nIf \\(c\\ge 2\\) then the smallest point on this line belonging to \\(S_n\\) is \\((1,c)\\) and the largest is \\((n+1-c,c)\\); there are \\(n+1-c\\le n-1\\) such points.  \nIf \\(c=1\\) the line is \\(y=1\\); the points of \\(S_n\\) on it are \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots,(n,1)\\), again \\(n\\) points, but the point \\((1,1)\\) also lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\) and therefore belongs to the anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_2\\). By Lemma\u202f1 a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis cannot contain another point of \\(D_2\\); consequently the total number of distinct points of \\(S_n\\) on this line is at most \\(n-1\\).  \n\nThe same argument applies to lines parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and to lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). \u220e\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 3  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line meets each anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_c\\) in at most one point, and there are exactly \\(n\\) anti\u2011diagonals intersecting \\(S_n\\) (those with \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\)). Hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on a sunny line. \u220e\n\n---\n\n#### Main Inequality  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\), with exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.  \nLet \\(I\\) denote the total number of incidences (ordered pairs \\((\\ell,P)\\) where line \\(\\ell\\) contains point \\(P\\in S_n\\)).  \nEach point of \\(S_n\\) must be incident to at least one line, so  \n\n\\[\nI\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, by Lemma\u202f2 each of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines contributes at most \\(n-1\\) incidences, and by Lemma\u202f3 each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines contributes at most \\(n\\) incidences. Hence  \n\n\\[\nI\\le (n-k)(n-1)+k\\,n = n^{2}-n-k+kn = n^{2}- (n-k). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1) and (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-(n-k),\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge \\frac{n-2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer, we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\ge \\left\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rceil\\, }.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Conclusion  \n\nFor any admissible configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering the set \\(S_n\\), the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\;\\left\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rceil,\\;\n\\left\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the set of possible \\(k\\) is contained in the interval \\([\\lceil (n-2)/2\\rceil ,\\, n]\\). Determining whether every integer in this interval actually occurs (i.e., constructing configurations attaining each such \\(k\\)) remains an open problem."
