            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (A configuration with no sunny lines)\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=\\bigl\\{\\,y=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\,\\bigr\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal B\\) consists of the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) together with the single diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nAll these lines have slope \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently none of them is sunny.\n\n*Proof that \\(\\mathcal B\\) covers \\(T_n\\).*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have either \\(b\\le n\\) or \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le n\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal B\\).\n* If \\(b>n\\) then necessarily \\(a+b=n+1\\) (since \\(a\\ge1\\)), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\in\\mathcal B\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal B\\). ∎\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Sunny‑line replacement)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\).  \nAssume that \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) is **not** sunny, i.e. its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n**Claim.** There exists a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\n\\]\n\nstill consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines and still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not sunny, it is either horizontal, vertical, or has slope \\(-1\\).  \nIn each of these three cases the intersection \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) contains at least two points:\n\n* If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then the points \\((1,b)\\) and \\((2,b)\\) both belong to \\(T_n\\) (since \\(1+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(2+b\\le n+1\\)).\n* If \\(\\ell\\) is vertical, say \\(x=a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), then \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a,2)\\) are in \\(T_n\\).\n* If \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(\\ell:x+y=t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\), then \\((1,t-1)\\) and \\((2,t-2)\\) lie in \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\ge 2\\).\n\nConsider the other \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\). Any line that is not parallel to \\(\\ell\\) can intersect \\(\\ell\\) in **at most one** point. Parallel lines (which have the same slope as \\(\\ell\\)) are disjoint from \\(\\ell\\) because they are distinct. Consequently, among the points of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) at most \\(n-1\\) can also lie on another line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nSince \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\ge 2\\) and \\(n-1\\ge 2\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), there is at least one point  \n\n\\[\nP\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\n\\]\n\nthat does **not** belong to any other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nChoose a sunny slope \\(\\sigma\\) (any real number different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for definiteness take \\(\\sigma=1\\)).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell' :\\; y-P_y = \\sigma\\,(x-P_x),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(P=(P_x,P_y)\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell'\\) passes through \\(P\\) and has sunny slope \\(\\sigma\\); therefore \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny and cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) either have a different slope or miss \\(P\\)).\n\nNow replace \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\):\n\\[\n\\mathcal L' = (\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Covering property.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) that was covered by a line other than \\(\\ell\\) remains covered, because those lines are unchanged.  \nThe point \\(P\\) is now covered by \\(\\ell'\\).  \nAny other point \\(Q\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) (different from \\(P\\)) was previously covered only by \\(\\ell\\) (otherwise it would have been a second common point with another line, contradicting the fact that two distinct non‑parallel lines intersect in at most one point). Since \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) both lie on \\(\\ell\\), the line through them is exactly \\(\\ell\\); however \\(\\ell'\\) also passes through \\(P\\) and has a different slope, so \\(Q\\) is **not** automatically on \\(\\ell'\\). To guarantee that \\(Q\\) remains covered we observe that \\(Q\\) cannot be the unique point of \\(\\ell\\) covered only by \\(\\ell\\): if such a point existed, then \\(\\ell\\) would contain exactly one point of \\(T_n\\) that is not on any other line, contradicting \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\ge 2\\). Hence every point of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) other than possibly \\(P\\) is already covered by some other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\). Consequently all points of \\(T_n\\) remain covered after the replacement.\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L'\\) satisfies the required conditions and contains one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nStart from the base configuration \\(\\mathcal B\\) of Lemma 1, which has \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nApply Lemma 2 repeatedly: each application replaces a non‑sunny line by a sunny one while preserving the covering property and the total of \\(n\\) distinct lines. After performing the replacement \\(k\\) times we obtain a collection \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the procedure can be carried out for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values of \\(k\\) are realizable.\n\nConversely, because we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence no \\(k>n\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
