ey would intersect \\(\\ell\\) in the same point). But the only lines parallel to \\(\\ell\\) that belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\) are the other horizontal (or vertical, or diagonal) lines, and there is at most one such parallel line in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (otherwise two parallel lines would coincide, contradicting distinctness). Hence at most one of the two points can be covered by another line, and the other point is uncovered.\n\nThus **in every case** there exists a point  \n\n\\[\nP\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\qquad\\text{that does not lie on any line of }\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nChoose a sunny slope \\(\\sigma\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (for definiteness take \\(\\sigma=1\\)).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell' :\\; y-P_y = \\sigma\\,(x-P_x),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(P=(P_x,P_y)\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell'\\) passes through \\(P\\) and has a sunny slope, so \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny and cannot coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) either have a different slope or miss \\(P\\)).\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L' = (\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  By construction \\(\\ell'\\) is different from every line remaining in \\(\\mathcal L\\); thus \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \n- Every point of \\(T_n\\) that was covered by a line other than \\(\\ell\\) remains covered.  \n- The point \\(P\\) is now covered by \\(\\ell'\\).  \n- Any other point \\(Q\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) (different from \\(P\\)) was already covered by some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\): indeed, if \\(Q\\) were not, then both \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) would be uncovered, contradicting (2) which guarantees the existence of **exactly one** uncovered point.\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still covers all points of \\(T_n\\). Moreover the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L'\\) is one larger than in \\(\\mathcal L\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nStart with the configuration \\(\\mathcal B\\) of Lemma 1, which has \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nApply Lemma 2 repeatedly: each application replaces a non‑sunny line by a sunny one while preserving the covering property. After performing the replacement \\(k\\) times we obtain a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause the replacement can be carried out as long as a non‑sunny line remains, we can reach any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound\n\nThe problem stipulates that exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines must be used. Consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence no integer \\(k>n\\) is admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists are precisely those with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
