ation by enlarging the set of sunny lines: instead of using the points \\((i,1)\\) we use \\((i,1)\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and for the remaining points of the first row we rely on the diagonal line \\(\\mathcal D\\) together with the horizontal lines \\(y=j\\) for \\(j\\ge2\\). A direct check shows that every point with \\(b=1\\) is indeed covered either by a sunny line (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(\\le k\\)) or by the diagonal line (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(n\\)); the only uncovered point would be \\((a,1)\\) with \\(k<a<n\\), but such a point satisfies \\(a\\le n-k-1\\) and therefore lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) which we have not used. To remedy this we simply *add* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) to the collection and delete the diagonal line \\(\\mathcal D\\) when \\(k<n\\).  \n\n   To keep the exposition clear we modify the construction as follows (the final version is given after the case analysis).\n\n   *Case 2: \\(b\\ge 2\\).*  \n   Then \\(b\\le n-k\\) because otherwise \\(a+b\\ge 1+(n-k+1)=n+2\\) would contradict \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n   Hence the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal H\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   *Case 3: \\(b=1\\) and \\(a=n\\).*  \n   The point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is part of the collection when \\(k<n\\).  \n\n   *Case 4: \\(b=1\\) and \\(a\\le k\\).*  \n   The point lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\n   *Case 5: \\(b=1\\) and \\(k<a<n\\).*  \n   In this sub‑case we have \\(a\\le n-k-1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  \n   Consequently the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) is *not* needed; instead we replace it by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{k+1}\\) (which passes through \\((k+1,1)\\)) and note that every point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(k<a\\le n-k-1\\) is already covered by the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) after a simple shift of indices. A more straightforward remedy is to **include the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in the set \\(\\mathcal H\\)** and, when we do so, we simply drop the diagonal line \\(\\mathcal D\\) (which is then unnecessary).  \n\n   After this small adjustment the collection of lines becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\ell_i : y-x = 1-i\\}_{i=1}^{k}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{horizontal}}\n   \\;}\n   \\]\n\n   (when \\(k=n\\) the second part is empty).  \n   The lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them have slope \\(1\\) (hence are sunny), and the remaining \\(n-k\\) have slope \\(0\\) (non‑sunny).  \n\n   **Coverage check for the final collection \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   * If \\(b\\le n-k\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)) contains the point.  \n   * If \\(b> n-k\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b=n-k+1\\) and consequently \\(a\\le k\\). In this situation the point lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\) (since \\(\\ell_a\\) passes through \\((a,1)\\) and has slope \\(1\\), it contains all points \\((a,\\,1+(t))\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\); in particular it contains \\((a,b)\\) because \\(b-1 = (n+1-a)-1 = n-a\\) equals the required vertical offset).  \n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   * For each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all required points.  \n   * No family can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines because only \\(n\\) lines are allowed.  \n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nThe problem requires *exactly* \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nTherefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\); i.e. \\(k>n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n#### 2.1. The lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\ell_i : y-x = 1-i\\}_{i=1}^{k}}_{\\text{sunny, slope }1}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{horizontal, slope }0}.\n\\]\n\n* The \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_i\\) have equation \\(y = x + (1-i)\\); each has slope \\(1\\), hence is sunny.  \n* The \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines have equation \\(y=j\\) with slope \\(0\\); none of them is sunny.  \n* All lines are distinct because the sunny lines have different intercepts and the horizontal lines have different \\(y\\)-coordinates, and a sunny line can never coincide with a horizontal line (different slopes).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n#### 2.2. Coverage of \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k\\).*  \nThen the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(b> n-k\\).*  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb>n-k\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;b\\ge n-k+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a\\le k .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(a\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\).  \nConsider the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\) whose equation is \\(y-x = 1-a\\).  \nBecause \\((a,1)\\) satisfies this equation, every point of the form \\((a,1+t)\\) with integer \\(t\\ge0\\) also satisfies it.  \nFor our point we have  \n\n\\[\nb-1 = (a+b)- (a+1) \\le (n+1)-(a+1)=n-a .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b-1\\) is a non‑negative integer not exceeding \\(n-a\\); consequently \\((a,b)=(a,1+(b-1))\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\nIn both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \nTherefore every element of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Existence for all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction above works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); it provides a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Non‑existence for \\(k>n\\)\n\nAs noted in Section 1, at most \\(n\\) lines are allowed, so we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. Hence no configuration exists for \\(k>n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Final answer\n\nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists are precisely those with  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n}.\n\\]"
