nsists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n#### 2.2. Coverage of \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k\\).*  \nThen the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(b> n-k\\).*  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb>n-k\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;b\\ge n-k+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a\\le k .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(a\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\).  \nConsider the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\) whose equation is \\(y-x = 1-a\\).  \nBecause \\((a,1)\\) satisfies this equation, every point of the form \\((a,1+t)\\) with integer \\(t\\ge0\\) also satisfies it.  \nFor our point we have  \n\n\\[\nb-1 = (a+b)- (a+1) \\le (n+1)-(a+1)=n-a .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(b-1\\) is a non‑negative integer not exceeding \\(n-a\\); consequently \\((a,b)=(a,1+(b-1))\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\nIn both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \nTherefore every element of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Existence for all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction above works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); it provides a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Non‑existence for \\(k>n\\)\n\nAs noted in Section 1, at most \\(n\\) lines are allowed, so we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. Hence no configuration exists for \\(k>n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Final answer\n\nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists are precisely those with  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification lo