            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nOnly \\(n\\) distinct lines are permitted, therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nHence \\(k>n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nDefine two families of lines.\n\n*Sunny lines.* For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-x = n+1-2i\\qquad\\bigl(\\text{equivalently }y = x + (n+1-2i)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); consequently it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\). Thus every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny. The \\(k\\) lines are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n*Horizontal (non‑sunny) lines.* For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-k\\) set  \n\n\\[\nh_j:\\; y=j .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(h_j\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so none of them is sunny. They are distinct because the \\(y\\)-coordinates differ.\n\nCollect all these lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{\\ell_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\cup\\{h_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n#### Case 1: \\(b\\le n-k\\).\n\nThe horizontal line \\(h_b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and clearly contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n#### Case 2: \\(b> n-k\\).\n\nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb>n-k\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;b\\ge n-k+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a\\le k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le a\\le k\\).  \nConsider the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\), whose equation is  \n\n\\[\ny = x + (n+1-2a).\n\\]\n\nPlugging \\(x=a\\) gives the point \\((a,\\,n+1-a)\\) on \\(\\ell_a\\).  \nBecause the line has slope \\(1\\), moving one unit upward in the \\(y\\)-direction also moves one unit rightward in the \\(x\\)-direction; consequently every point of the form  \n\n\\[\n(a+t,\\;n+1-a+t),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\n\nlies on \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nt = b-(n+1-a).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(t\\le0\\); and because \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(a\\le k\\), we have \\(t\\ge a-k\\ge - (k-1)\\), so \\(t\\) is an integer.  \nWith this choice of \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\n(a+t,\\;n+1-a+t) = (a,b),\n\\]\n\nhence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all required lattice points.  \nNo configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,