(1\\le a\\le n\\), the number of admissible values of \\(a\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\). Since \\(q\\ge 2\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\)), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3.3}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) actually attains the bound \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); indeed its points in \\(T_n\\) are \\((a,a)\\) with \\(2a\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a=1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), and together with the possible point \\((\\frac{n+2}{2},\\frac{n}{2})\\) when \\(n\\) is even we obtain exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.  \n   Thus **every sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).**\n\n4. **Counting argument for a family containing sunny lines.**  \n\n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and let exactly \\(k\\) of them be sunny.\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) can intersect the triangle \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points (the whole side of the triangle). Hence each of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   By (3.3) each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\n\n   Therefore the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl|\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\ell\\cap T_n\\bigr|\n   \\le (n-k)\\,n + k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   = n^{2} - k\\Bigl(n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The covering condition requires this quantity to be at least \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}