even \\(n\\ge4\\) we already have \\(k\\le2\\); together with \\(k\\le n-3\\) this forces \\(k\\le2\\).  \n   For odd \\(n\\ge5\\) we use the fact that a sunny line of slope different from \\(-1\\) can intersect the hypotenuse \\(a+b=n+1\\) in **at most one** lattice point (because points on that line satisfy \\(a+b\\) constant, and the slope condition forces the two coordinates to change together). Hence the \\(n-3\\) sunny lines can together cover at most \\(n-3\\) points of the hypotenuse, while the hypotenuse itself contains exactly \\(n\\) lattice points. The remaining at least three points of the hypotenuse must be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); but there is only **one** such line in the family (otherwise two of them would be parallel, contradicting distinctness). Therefore a configuration with any sunny line is impossible for odd \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n   The only remaining case is \\(n=3\\). Direct inspection shows that the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with two non\u2011sunny lines without violating distinctness; the only feasible arrangement is the three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\), i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The arguments above show that any configuration satisfying the covering condition must contain **no** sunny line. The construction with the \\(n\\) horizontal lines demonstrates that \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n\\) consider the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nh_j:\\; y=j .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(h_j\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(h_b\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{h_1,\\dots ,h_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers every point of \\(T_n\\). Consequently \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points of \\(T_n\\) that a sunny line can contain\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope \\(m\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nm\\neq0,\\qquad m\\neq\\infty,\\qquad m\\neq-1 .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).\n\n*If \\(m\\) is irrational*, the equation \\(b=ma+c\\) with integers \\(a,b\\) can have at most one solution; otherwise two different integer solutions would give a rational value for \\(m\\).\n\n*If \\(m\\) is rational*, write \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \nFrom \\(b=\\frac{p}{q}a+c\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nqb = pa + qc .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nSince the left\u2011hand side is an integer, the right\u2011hand side must also be an integer; therefore \\(qc\\) is an integer. Put \\(qc=r\\in\\mathbb Z\\); (2.2) becomes  \n\n\\[\nqb = pa + r .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nReducing (2.3) modulo \\(q\\) yields \\(pa\\equiv -r\\pmod q\\). Because \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), this congruence determines a **single residue class** of admissible \\(a\\) modulo \\(q\\). Hence the \\(x\\)-coordinates of the integer points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the line belong to one residue class modulo \\(q\\).\n\nWith \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the number of such \\(a\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\). As \\(q\\ge2\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\)), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) actually attains this bound: its points in \\(T_n\\) are \\((a,a)\\) with \\(2a\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a=1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), together with the point \\((\\frac{n+2}{2},\\frac{n}{2})\\) when \\(n\\) is even. Hence (2.4) is sharp.\n\nThus **any sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Counting points covered by a family containing sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\), and let exactly \\(k\\) of them be sunny.\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) can intersect the triangle \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points (the whole side of the triangle). Hence each of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nBy (2.4) each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\ell\\cap T_n\\bigr|\n\\le (n-k)n + k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n= n^{2} - k\\Bigl(n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr).\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nSince the left\u2011hand side must be at least \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nn^{2} - k\\Bigl(n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr)\n\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nSolving (3.2) for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}}\n     {\\,n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,}\n= \\frac{n(n-1)/2}\n     {\\,n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,}.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Necessity of the three forbidden directions\n\nThe three families of non\u2011sunny lines each cover a whole side of the triangle:\n\n* horizontal lines \\(y=j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n\\)) cover the side \\(b=1\\);\n* vertical lines \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)) cover the side \\(a=1\\);\n* lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)), cover the hypotenuse \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) omitted a line of one of these three directions, the points on the corresponding side would have no line passing through them, contradicting the covering requirement. Consequently at least one line of each of the three directions must belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3 .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final elimination of sunny lines\n\nCombine the general bound (3.3) with (4.1).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even, write \\(n=2m\\) (\\(m\\ge2\\)).  \nThen \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and  \n\n\\[\nn-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor = m-1 .\n\\]\n\nFormula (3.3) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{2m(2m-1)/2}{m-1}=2+\\frac{1}{m-1}<3 .\n\\]\n\nTogether with \\(k\\le n-3=2m-3\\) we obtain \\(k\\le2\\).  \nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(k\\ge1\\). Then at most two of the three required directions are represented by non\u2011sunny lines, leaving at least one side of the triangle uncovered\u2014a contradiction. Hence \\(k=0\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd, write \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  \nNow \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and  \n\n\\[\nn-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor = m .\n\\]\n\nFrom (3.3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{(2m+1)2m/2}{m}=2m+1=n .\n\\]\n\nThe restriction (4.1) gives \\(k\\le n-3=2m-2\\).  \nIf \\(k\\ge1\\), then at most two of the three forbidden directions appear among the non\u2011sunny lines. Consider the hypotenuse \\(a+b=n+1\\), which contains exactly \\(n\\) lattice points. A sunny line can meet this hypotenuse in **at most one** lattice point (otherwise two distinct points of the hypotenuse would have the same sum of coordinates, forcing the line to be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting sunniness). Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\le n-3\\) points of the hypotenuse, leaving at least three points uncovered. Since there is only one non\u2011sunny line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), those remaining points cannot be covered \u2013 a contradiction. Therefore \\(k=0\\).\n\nThe remaining small case \\(n=3\\) can be checked directly; the only possible covering uses the three horizontal lines, so again \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nA covering of all points of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines must contain **no** sunny line. The construction with the \\(n\\) horizontal lines shows that \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]"
