oor .\n   \\tag{S1}\n   \\]\n\n   The bound is sharp (e.g. the line \\(y=x\\) attains it).\n\n3. **Maximum number of points of \\(T_n\\) that a non‑sunny line can contain.**  \n   A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) can intersect the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points – namely the whole side of the triangle that lies on that direction. Hence each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Using the estimates of steps 2 and 3 and ignoring possible overlaps (which can only *decrease* the total number of covered points) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|\n   \\le (n-k)\\,n + k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{U1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) and solving for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}}\n        {\\,n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,}\n   =\\frac{n(n-1)/2}{\\,n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,}.\n   \\tag{U2}\n   \\]\n\n   A short case analysis yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{U3}\n   \\]\n\n   (For even \\(n=2m\\) the denominator equals \\(m-1\\) and (U2) gives \\(k\\le 2m-2=n-2\\); for odd \\(n=2m+1\\) the denominator equals \\(m\\) and (U2) gives \\(k\\le 2m-1=n-2\\).)\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Step 5.1 – non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Choose the following \\(n-k-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k-1\n   \\tag{C1}\n   \\]\n\n   and the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1 .\n   \\tag{C2}\n   \\]\n\n   The lines in (C1) together with (C2) are all non‑sunny; they are distinct and there are exactly \\((n-k-1)+1=n-k\\) of them.\n\n   *Step 5.2 – points already covered.*  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k-1\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); every point with \\(b=1\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\). Hence all points whose first coordinate is at most \\(n-k-1\\) or whose second coordinate equals \\(1\\) are already covered.\n\n   *Step 5.3 – the remaining lattice points.*  \n   The set of points that are still uncovered is  \n\n   \\[\n   R=\n   \\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k\\ \\text{and}\\ b\\ge 2\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le k+1\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_t:\\; y = -x + (n+1+t) .\n   \\tag{C3}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) is \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_t\\) is **not** sunny.  \n   However, we need exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, so we replace the first \\(k\\) of the lines \\(\\ell_t\\) by sunny lines that pass through the same points.  \n\n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i:\\; y = \\frac{1}{i}\\,x + \\bigl(n+1 -\\tfrac{1}{i}(n-k)\\bigr) .\n   \\tag{C4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{1}{i}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(s_i\\) is sunny.  \n   Direct substitution shows that the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i =\\bigl(n-k+i,\\; i+1\\bigr)\n   \\]\n\n   satisfies the equation of \\(s_i\\); thus \\(P_i\\in s_i\\).  \n   Moreover, the points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) are distinct and all belong to the set \\(R\\).\n\n   *Step 5.4 – covering the rest of \\(R\\).*  \n   The remaining points of \\(R\\) (those not equal to any \\(P_i\\)) lie on the lines \\(\\ell_{k+1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\), which are non‑sunny and distinct from the lines already chosen. There are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)-(k+1)+1 = n-k-1\n   \\]\n\n   such lines, and together with the vertical and horizontal lines from Step 5.1 we obtain a total of  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k-1) \\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{horizontal})\\;+\\;(n-k-1)\\;(\\text{non‑sunny }\\ell)\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny }s)\n   = n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.\n\n   By construction every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines, and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\) are sunny. Hence a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge n-1\\).**  \n   Suppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and contains \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines. Then at most one line is non‑sunny. A non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (step 3). Each sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points (step 2). Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n + (n-1)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) this quantity is **strictly smaller** than \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) (a direct verification of the inequality). Consequently the covering condition cannot be satisfied when \\(k\\ge n-1\\).\n\n   Therefore no configuration exists for \\(k\\ge n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Conclusion**  \nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-2}.\n\\]\n\nThe construction in step 5 shows that each such \\(k\\) is attainable, and the counting argument in step 6 proves that larger values of \\(k\\) are impossible.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
