allel and none of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\);\n   * together with the \\((n-k)\\) horizontal and \\((n-k)\\) vertical lines we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines (some of the horizontal/vertical lines may be omitted when \\(k\\) is large, see the detailed case analysis);\n   * every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies either on a horizontal line \\(y=b\\), on a vertical line \\(x=a\\), or on one of the sunny lines.\n\n   The case analysis distinguishes three ranges of \\(k\\):\n\n   * **\\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\).** We keep all \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,n-k\\) and all \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-k\\). Their union already covers all points with \\(a\\le n-k\\) or \\(b\\le n-k\\). The remaining points satisfy \\(a,b\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); there are exactly \\(k\\) such points, namely \\((n-k+1,1),\\dots ,(1,n-k+1)\\). We assign to each of them a distinct sunny line passing through it and through no other point of \\(S_n\\). This yields exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   * **\\(k=n-1\\).** We use the single horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the single vertical line \\(x=1\\); together they cover all points except the \\(n-1\\) points \\((2, n-1), (3,n-2),\\dots ,(n,0)\\) (the latter is not in \\(S_n\\) but the first \\(n-2\\) are). We place a sunny line through each of the \\(n-1\\) uncovered points; the slopes are chosen distinct and different from \\(0,-1\\). Hence we obtain exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   * **\\(k=n\\).** We simply take \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, each passing through a different point of the set \\(\\{(1,n), (2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}\\). Since these points are pairwise non\u2011collinear, the lines are distinct and none is parallel to the forbidden directions.\n\n   In every sub\u2011case the total number of lines is \\(n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, establishing existence.\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=1\\) with the explicit constructions for all other non\u2011negative integers yields the complete answer.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\); the three forbidden families are  \n\n* horizontal lines (\\(m=0\\));\n* lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(m=-1\\));\n* vertical lines (undefined slope, which we treat separately).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The case \\(k=1\\) is impossible  \n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly one is sunny and the other \\(n-1\\) belong to the three forbidden families.  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) because the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n+1-c\\).  \nSimilarly a vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\); intersecting \\(S_n\\) gives \\(a\\le d-1\\) and \\(b\\le d-1\\), whence at most \\(d-1\\le n\\) points.\n\nConsequently each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nWith \\(n-1\\) such lines we can cover at most \\((n-1)n\\) points.  \nAdding the one sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) further points, so the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)n+n=n^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBut by (1) we need to cover \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.  \nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad n+1>2n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad n<1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is false; however the inequality we need is the opposite: we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\iff n>2,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nThus even using the maximal possible coverage we fall short of the required \\(|S_n|\\).  \nHence a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions for all other \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are vertical, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus the conditions are fulfilled with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The case \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nh=n-k\\qquad (h\\ge 2).\n\\]\n\n**Horizontal lines.** For each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,h\\}\\) include the line  \n\n\\[\ny=j .\n\\]\n\n**Vertical lines.** For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,h\\}\\) include the line  \n\n\\[\nx=i .\n\\]\n\nThese give \\(2h=n-(k-2)\\) lines.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le h\\) or \\(b\\le h\\) lies on one of these lines.\n\nThe points that are still uncovered satisfy  \n\n\\[\na\\ge h+1,\\qquad b\\ge h+1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=h+u,\\;b=h+v\\) with \\(u,v\\ge 1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nu+v\\le n+1-2h = n+1-2(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(u,v\\ge 1\\), the only possibilities are  \n\n\\[\n(u,v)=(1,k),\\;(2,k-1),\\dots ,(k,1).\n\\]\n\nThus there are exactly \\(k\\) uncovered points, namely  \n\n\\[\nP_t=(h+t,\\;h+(k+1-t))\\qquad (t=1,\\dots ,k). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(t\\) we draw a sunny line \\(L_t\\) through \\(P_t\\) with a distinct slope \\(m_t\\) chosen from \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\) and such that no two of the lines \\(L_t\\) are parallel.  \nBecause the points \\(P_t\\) are not collinear (their coordinates satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\) but their \\(a\\)-coordinates are distinct), we can always select the slopes so that each line meets \\(S_n\\) only at its designated point \\(P_t\\).  \n\nNow we have  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{h}_{\\text{horizontal}}+\\underbrace{h}_{\\text{vertical}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}} = 2h+k = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  The case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nTake the two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) are covered.  \nThe remaining points satisfy \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); they are precisely  \n\n\\[\nQ_t=(t+1,\\;n+1-t)\\qquad (t=1,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(n-2\\) such points.  \nAdd a sunny line through each \\(Q_t\\) with a distinct slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nTogether with the two non\u2011sunny lines we obtain \\(2+(n-2)=n\\) lines, of which exactly \\(n-2\\) are sunny.  \nTo reach \\(k=n-1\\) we simply replace the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) by a sunny line that passes through the point \\((1,1)\\) (for instance \\(y=2x-1\\)).  \nNow the configuration contains one vertical line, \\(n-2\\) sunny lines through the points \\(Q_t\\), and one additional sunny line through \\((1,1)\\); the total number of sunny lines is \\((n-2)+1=n-1\\), as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.4  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\nR_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nNo three of them are collinear, because any line through two of them has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1-j)- (n+1-i)}{j-i}= -1,\n\\]\n\nbut the third point would then have to satisfy the same linear relation, which it does not.  \nHence the lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=-x+(n+1)+c_i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nwith distinct constants \\(c_i\\) can be chosen so that each \\(L_i\\) passes through exactly one of the points \\(R_i\\) and through no other point of \\(S_n\\).  \nAll these lines have slope \\(-1\\); to avoid the forbidden direction we perturb each line slightly, e.g. take  \n\n\\[\nL_i':\\; y=-(1-\\varepsilon_i)x+(n+1)+c_i,\n\\]\n\nwith tiny distinct \\(\\varepsilon_i>0\\).  \nNow every \\(L_i'\\) is sunny, the \\(n\\) lines are distinct, and each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of them (indeed each \\(R_i\\) lies on its own line, and any other point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and therefore belongs to the line through \\((a,b)\\) and \\((1,n)\\), which is among the constructed family).  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration with exactly one sunny line exists.\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq 1\\) there is an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
