hem a distinct sunny line.  Altogether we have \\(1\\) non\u2011sunny line and \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n   * **\\(k=n\\).** For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) take the point \\(P_i=(i,n+1-i)\\).  Choose a distinct slope \\(m_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and define the line \\(L_i:\\;y=m_i x+b_i\\) with \\(b_i\\) chosen so that \\(L_i\\) passes through \\(P_i\\).  The \\(n\\) lines are distinct, all are sunny, and each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of them (indeed each \\(P_i\\) lies on its own line, and any other \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) coincides with some \\(P_i\\)).  \n\n   In every sub\u2011case the required number of lines is exactly \\(n\\) and the number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=1\\) with the explicit constructions for all other \\(k\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\); the three forbidden families are  \n\n* horizontal lines (\\(m=0\\));\n* lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(m=-1\\));\n* vertical lines (undefined slope).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  No configuration with exactly one sunny line  \n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly one is sunny and the other \\(n-1\\) belong to the three forbidden families.\n\n* A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\): the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n+1-c\\le n\\).\n* A vertical line \\(x=c\\) is analogous and also contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n* A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\).  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(d\\le n+1\\), and the integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) of \\(a+b=d\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) are at most \\(d-1\\le n\\).\n\nHence each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nWith \\(n-1\\) such lines we can cover at most \\((n-1)n\\) points.  \nThe single sunny line can add at most another \\(n\\) points, so the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)n+n=n^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we need to cover \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.  \nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad n+1>2n\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad n<1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is false; the correct inequality is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\iff n>2,\n\\]\n\nand this holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nThus (2) is insufficient to cover all points of \\(S_n\\); a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for each admissible \\(k\\), a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non\u2011sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Hence the requirements are met with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nPut  \n\n\\[\nh=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }h\\ge 2).\n\\]\n\n**Horizontal lines.** For each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,h\\}\\) include the line \\(y=j\\).\n\n**Vertical lines.** For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,h\\}\\) include the line \\(x=i\\).\n\nThese give \\(2h\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le h\\) or \\(b\\le h\\) lies on one of them.\n\nThe points still uncovered satisfy  \n\n\\[\na\\ge h+1,\\qquad b\\ge h+1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1