inct slopes \\(m_i\\) with \\(m_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (for example \\(m_i=i\\)).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=m_i x + b_i,\\qquad b_i\\text{ chosen so that }L_i\\text{ passes through }R_i .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(L_i\\) are sunny and distinct because their slopes are distinct.  \nEach point \\(R_i\\) lies on its own line \\(L_i\\); any other point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) coincides with some \\(R_i\\) (the set \\(\\{R_i\\}\\) is exactly the set of points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\), and the remaining points of \\(S_n\\) have smaller sum and are already covered by the lines \\(L_i\\) because those lines extend beyond the single point).  Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Final answer  \n\nNo configuration with exactly one sunny line exists, while for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nthere is an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
