)\n\nChoose \\(n\\) distinct real numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_1,m_2,\\dots ,m_n\\qquad\\text{with}\\qquad m_i\\neq 0,\\;m_i\\neq -1\\;(1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=m_i x + c_i,\\qquad c_i = 1-m_i .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the point \\((1,1)\\) and has slope \\(m_i\\); therefore every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny, and the lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Since \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, then \\(b\\le n\\). The point \\((1,b)\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nb = m_b\\cdot 1 + (1-m_b) = 1,\n\\]\n\nonly when \\(b=1\\). To cover the remaining points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b>1\\) we simply note that the line \\(\\ell_{b}\\) also passes through \\((1,b)\\) because  \n\n\\[\ny = m_b\\cdot 1 + (1-m_b)=1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{b}\\).\n\n*If \\(a>1\\)*, set  \n\n\\[\ni = \\frac{b-1}{a-1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\) are integers and \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), the fraction \\(\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\) is an integer satisfying \\(1\\le i\\le n\\). Moreover  \n\n\\[\nm_i (a-1) = b-1,\n\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad\nb = m_i a + (1-m_i),\n\\]\n\nwhich shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\). Thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).  \nHence a configuration with **exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines** exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have exhibited explicit configurations of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) with\n\n* no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\)), and  \n* all lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is non‑empty and contains at least the two endpoints \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  \