\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \n   Hence all points are covered and exactly \\(k=0\\) lines are sunny.\n\nCombining the impossibility for \\(k\\ge1\\) with the explicit construction for \\(k=0\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) contains the lattice points of the right‑angled triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Sunny versus non‑sunny lines  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is *not* parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\ny=0,\\qquad x=0,\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence a line is **non‑sunny** precisely when its slope belongs to  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the line is respectively horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A sunny line can meet at most one point of \\(S_n\\)\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\\text{If a line } \\ell \\text{ is sunny, then } |\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points\n\\((a,b),(c,d)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\neq c\\).  \nSet \\(\\Delta x=c-a\\) and \\(\\Delta y=d-b\\); then the slope of \\(\\ell\\) is \\(\\Delta y/\\Delta x\\).\n\nBecause \\((a,b),(c,d)\\in S_n\\),\n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1,\\qquad c+d\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAdding these inequalities gives  \n\n\\[\n(a+c)+(b+d)\\le 2n+2 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\Delta x,\\Delta y\\) are integers with \\(|\\Delta x|,|\\Delta y|\\le n\\), the reduced fraction\n\\(\\Delta y/\\Delta x\\) can have denominator at most \\(n\\).  \nIf the slope were different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), then after reducing the fraction we would obtain a\npair of coprime integers \\((p,q)\\) with \\(|p|,|q|\\le n\\) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).\n\nFrom (2) we have  \n\n\\[\n|a-c|+|b-d|=|\\Delta x|+|\\Delta y|\\le n .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the only possible reduced slopes arising from two points of \\(S_n\\) are\n\\(0\\) (when \\(\\Delta y=0\\)), \\(\\infty\\) (when \\(\\Delta x=0\\)), or \\(-1\\) (when \\(\\Delta y=-\\Delta x\\)).\nAll three correspond to non‑sunny directions, contradicting the assumption that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound on the total number of covered points\n\nSuppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy the lemma, the sunny lines together can cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nEach of the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines is either horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\); in any case a single such line is determined by a single constant (its \\(y\\)-intercept, its \\(x\\)-intercept, or the value of \\(x+y\\)).  \nThus the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can collectively contain at most \\(n-k\\) *different* constants, and consequently at most \\(n-k\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nHence the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L} (L\\cap S_n)|\\le k+(n-k)=n .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Comparison with the required number of points\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n .\n\\]\n\nTherefore (3) shows that **any** configuration containing at least one sunny line (\\(k\\ge1\\)) cannot cover all points of \\(S_n\\); it would miss at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n>0\\) points.  \n\nConsequently, the covering condition can be fulfilled only when \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are pairwise distinct, none is sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), and for any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \nHence every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered, and exactly \\(k=0\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe only non‑negative integer \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\nThis holds for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\)."
