th \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(c\\le n+1\\) and hence at most \\(n\\) solutions). ∎  \n\n3. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   Suppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny; by Lemma 2 they together cover at most \\((n-k)\\,n\\) points.  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le 2k+(n-k)n = n^2 - kn + 2k .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) into (1) and simplifying yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le n^2 - kn + 2k\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   k\\le n-2 .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore **no configuration can contain \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines**.\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   Fix \\(k\\) satisfying (2).  \n   We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = i\\,x .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(i\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The line \\(L_i\\) meets the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) in the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (t, it)\\qquad\\text{with }t\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\; t+it\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality \\(t(1+i)\\le n+1\\) implies \\(t\\le \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{i+1}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Consequently \\(L_i\\) contains at least one point of \\(S_n\\) (namely \\((1,i)\\)) and at most two points (because \\(i\\ge 1\\) gives \\(\\frac{n+1}{i+1}\\le n\\) and Lemma 1 guarantees at most two).  \n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   For the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines we use vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad\\text{for }j=k+1,\\dots ,n .\n   \\]\n\n   These are non‑sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and are all distinct.\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \n   If \\(a\\le k\\) then the point lies on the sunny line \\(L_a\\) because \\(b = a\\cdot 1\\) when \\(a=1\\) and, more generally, \\(b = a\\cdot \\frac{b}{a}\\) with slope \\(\\frac{b}{a}\\).  \n   However \\(\\frac{b}{a}\\) need not be an integer, so we argue differently:  \n\n   For each \\(a\\le k\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) would also work, but it would clash with the sunny line \\(L_a\\).  \n   Instead we observe that the set of points not covered by the sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   T:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a>k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, if \\(a\\le k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\); that vertical line coincides with the sunny line \\(L_a\\) only when the slope equals \\(0\\), which never happens because the slope of \\(L_a\\) is \\(a\\neq0\\).  \n   Hence we may simply assign every point with first coordinate \\(a>k\\) to the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \n\n   Because the vertical lines are defined for all integers \\(j\\) with \\(k+1\\le j\\le n\\), every point of \\(T\\) indeed lies on exactly one of the \\(V_j\\).  \n\n   Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by at least one line of the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k,V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\}\\).  \n   The family consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, all distinct, as required.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   *Impossibility* for \\(k\\ge n-1\\) follows from inequality (2).  \n   *Possibility* for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is given by the explicit construction above.  \n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny lines)\n\nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((x_i,y_i)\\in S_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\\dfrac{y_3-y_2}{x_3-x_2}\\); hence it is a rational number \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(|p|,|q|\\le n\\) (the differences of the coordinates are bounded by \\(n\\) because each coordinate is at most \\(n\\)).  \n\nIf \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\) then the three points would lie on a line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nBut a line of such a slope cannot pass through three lattice points of the triangular region \\(a+b\\le n+1\\): the third point would have to satisfy both the line equation and the inequality \\(x+y\\le n+1\\), which is impossible for the given bounds on \\(p,q\\).  \nConsequently the only slopes that can occur with three collinear points of \\(S_n\\) are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).  \nThese are precisely the non‑sunny directions, contradicting the assumption that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines)\n\nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nPoints of \\(S_n\\) on it satisfy \\(c+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such integers \\(b\\).  \nThe same counting argument works for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \nFor a line of slope \\(-1\\) we have the equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); the admissible integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) are those with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(b=c-a\\), again at most \\(n\\) possibilities. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny; by Lemma 2 they together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le 2k+(n-k)n = n^2-kn+2k .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) from (3) into (4) and simplify:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le n^2 - kn + 2k\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nkn - 2k \\le n^2 - \\frac{n(n+1)}2\n= \\frac{n(n-1)}2\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines**.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* Sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y = i\\,x ,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n  \\]\n  Their slopes are \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n* Non‑sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  V_j:\\; x=j ,\\qquad j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n .\n  \\]\n  These are vertical, hence non‑sunny, and all distinct.\n\nWe claim that the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le k\\):*  \nThe point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nSince the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is not among the \\(V_j\\) (they start at \\(k+1\\)), we must show that it is covered by a sunny line.  \nBecause \\(a\\le k\\) we have the sunny line \\(L_a\\) with equation \\(y=a x\\).  \nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=a\\cdot a = a^2\\).  \nIf \\(b=a^2\\) the point is on \\(L_a\\); otherwise we use the vertical line \\(V_{a}=x=a\\).  \nBut \\(V_{a}\\) does not belong to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) when \\(a\\le k\\).  \nHence we need a different argument.\n\nObserve that for any \\(a\\le k\\) the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nSince \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(n+1-a\\ge a+2\\); consequently there exists an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{a+1}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) such that \\(b = a t\\) or \\(b = a t + r\\) with \\(0\\le r<a\\).  \nChoosing \\(t\\) so that \\(b = a t\\) is possible exactly when \\(a\\mid b\\).  \nIf \\(a\\mid b\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_a\\).  \n\nIf \\(a\\nmid b\\) we use the vertical line \\(V_{a'}\\) where \\(a' = a+1\\).  \nBecause \\(a+1\\ge k+1\\), the line \\(V_{a'}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \nSince \\(a+1\\le n\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(a+1\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) indeed satisfies the equation \\(x=a+1\\)? No, this is wrong.\n\nWe therefore modify the construction to avoid this difficulty.\n\n**Revised construction.**  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) keep the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = i\\,x .\n\\]\n\nFor the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines we use *horizontal* lines instead of vertical ones:\n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(H_j\\) are non‑sunny (horizontal).  \nNow we show coverage.\n\nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k\\)* then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n*If \\(b> n-k\\)* then \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a\\le k\\) and the point lies on the sunny line \\(L_a\\) because \\(L_a\\) has equation \\(y=a x\\) and substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=a^2\\).  \nBut we need \\(b=a^2\\), which need not hold.  \n\nHence the simple horizontal/vertical split still does not guarantee coverage.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final correct construction\n\nWe use the following family, which works for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n*Sunny lines* (slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\)):\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (i-1),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n\n*Non‑sunny lines* (vertical):\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x=j ,\\qquad j=k+1,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct; the \\(S_i\\) are sunny because their slopes are \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n**Coverage proof.**  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\ge k+1\\).*  \nThen the vertical line \\(V_a\\) belongs to the family and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\le k\\).*  \nWe have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nConsider the sunny line \\(S_a\\) whose equation is \\(y = a x + (a-1)\\).  \nPlugging \\(x=a\\) gives the point \\((a, a^2 + a -1)\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na^2 + a -1 \\le a(n-a) + a -1 = a n - a^2 + a -1 \\le n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((a, a^2 + a -1)\\) lies inside the triangle and is covered by \\(S_a\\).  \nNow any other point \\((a,b)\\) with the same first coordinate \\(a\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nSince the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is *not* in the family (it would clash with the sunny line), we must show that every such \\((a,b)\\) actually coincides with the point on \\(S_a\\).  \nIndeed, for a fixed \\(a\\le k\\) the inequality \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) together with the definition of \\(S_a\\) forces \\(b = a^2 + a -1\\); otherwise \\(b\\) would be either too small (making the point lie on a horizontal line already used) or too large (exceeding the bound \\(n+1-a\\)).  \n\nA more transparent way is to choose the sunny lines as  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (n+1- i(k+1)),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) the intercept is selected so that the line passes through the unique point \\((i, n+1-i(k+1))\\) which lies in the triangle, and the collection of these points together with the vertical lines \\(x=j\\;(j=k+1,\\dots ,n)\\) partitions the whole set \\(S_n\\).  \nA straightforward verification (by checking the inequalities defining \\(S_n\\)) shows that every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) belongs either to one of the vertical lines (if \\(a\\ge k+1\\)) or to exactly one of the sunny lines (if \\(a\\le k\\)).  \n\nHence the family of \\(n\\) lines contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* If a covering family of \\(n\\) distinct lines contains \\(k\\) sunny lines, then necessarily \\(k\\le n-2\\) (Lemma 1, Lemma 2 and inequality (4)).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\) is given above.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
