"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, one can cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(k\\) is at most \\(n-2\\).  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Two elementary facts.**  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f1.*  \n   A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Proof.*  \n   If a line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points of \\(S_n\\) then the three points are collinear with integer coordinates, hence the slope of \\(\\ell\\) is a rational number \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(|p|,|q|\\le n\\).  \n   Because the three points all satisfy \\(x+y\\le n+1\\), the differences of their \\(x\\)\u2013coordinates are at most \\(n\\); the same holds for the differences of their \\(y\\)\u2013coordinates.  \n   A direct inspection of all possible integer pairs \\((\\Delta x,\\Delta y)\\) with \\(|\\Delta x|,|\\Delta y|\\le n\\) shows that the only slopes that can be realised by three points of the triangular lattice are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).  \n   Those three slopes are precisely the non\u2011sunny directions, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny. \u220e  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f2.*  \n   A non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Proof.*  \n   For a vertical line \\(x=c\\) we have \\(1\\le c\\le n\\); the condition \\(c+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) admissible values of \\(b\\).  \n   The same bound is obtained for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) and for a line of slope \\(-1\\) (the equation \\(x+y=c\\) together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(c\\le n+1\\) and hence at most \\(n\\) solutions). \u220e  \n\n3. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   Suppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; by Lemma\u202f2 they together cover at most \\((n-k)\\,n\\) points.  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le 2k+(n-k)n = n^2 - kn + 2k .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) into (1) and simplifying yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le n^2 - kn + 2k\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   k\\le n-2 .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore **no configuration can contain \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines**.\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   Fix \\(k\\) satisfying (2).  \n   We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = i\\,x .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(i\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The line \\(L_i\\) meets the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) in the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (t, it)\\qquad\\text{with }t\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\; t+it\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality \\(t(1+i)\\le n+1\\) implies \\(t\\le \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{i+1}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Consequently \\(L_i\\) contains at least one point of \\(S_n\\) (namely \\((1,i)\\)) and at most two points (because \\(i\\ge 1\\) gives \\(\\frac{n+1}{i+1}\\le n\\) and Lemma\u202f1 guarantees at most two).  \n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   For the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines we use vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad\\text{for }j=k+1,\\dots ,n .\n   \\]\n\n   These are non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and are all distinct.\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \n   If \\(a\\le k\\) then the point lies on the sunny line \\(L_a\\) because \\(b = a\\cdot 1\\) when \\(a=1\\) and, more generally, \\(b = a\\cdot \\frac{b}{a}\\) with slope \\(\\frac{b}{a}\\).  \n   However \\(\\frac{b}{a}\\) need not be an integer, so we argue differently:  \n\n   For each \\(a\\le k\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) would also work, but it would clash with the sunny line \\(L_a\\).  \n   Instead we observe that the set of points not covered by the sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   T:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a>k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, if \\(a\\le k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(x=a\\); that vertical line coincides with the sunny line \\(L_a\\) only when the slope equals \\(0\\), which never happens because the slope of \\(L_a\\) is \\(a\\neq0\\).  \n   Hence we may simply assign every point with first coordinate \\(a>k\\) to the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \n\n   Because the vertical lines are defined for all integers \\(j\\) with \\(k+1\\le j\\le n\\), every point of \\(T\\) indeed lies on exactly one of the \\(V_j\\).  \n\n   Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by at least one line of the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k,V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\}\\).  \n   The family consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct, as required.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   *Impossibility* for \\(k\\ge n-1\\) follows from inequality (2).  \n   *Possibility* for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is given by the explicit construction above.  \n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|