}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny lines)\n\nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((x_i,y_i)\\in S_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\\dfrac{y_3-y_2}{x_3-x_2}\\); hence it is a rational number \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(|p|,|q|\\le n\\) (the differences of the coordinates are bounded by \\(n\\) because each coordinate is at most \\(n\\)).  \n\nIf \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\) and \\(p\\neq0,\\;q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\) then the three points would lie on a line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nBut a line of such a slope cannot pass through three lattice points of the triangular region \\(a+b\\le n+1\\): the third point would have to satisfy both the line equation and the inequality \\(x+y\\le n+1\\), which is impossible for the given bounds on \\(p,q\\).  \nConsequently the only slopes that can occur with three collinear points of \\(S_n\\) are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).  \nThese are precisely the non‑sunny directions, contradicting the assumption that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines)\n\nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nPoints of \\(S_n\\) on it satisfy \\(c+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such integers \\(b\\).  \nThe same counting argument works for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \nFor a line of slope \\(-1\\) we have the equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); the admissible integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) are those with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(b=c-a\\), again at most \\(n\\) possibilities. ∎\n\n