 three points modulo the lattice \\(\\mathbb Z^2\\).  \nSubtracting the first point from the other two we obtain the vectors\n\\((\\Delta x_1,\\Delta y_1)=(x_2-x_1,y_2-y_1)\\) and\n\\((\\Delta x_2,\\Delta y_2)=(x_3-x_1,y_3-y_1)\\).\nBoth vectors are integer multiples of \\((q,p)\\) because they lie on the same line.\nHence \\(\\Delta x_i = q\\,t_i\\) and \\(\\Delta y_i = p\\,t_i\\) for some integers \\(t_i\\).\n\nSince \\(|\\Delta x_i|\\le n\\) and \\(|\\Delta y_i|\\le n\\), we have \\(|t_i|\\le \\frac{n}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\le n\\).\nConsequently the three points are of the form\n\\[\n(x_1,y_1),\\;(x_1+q t_1,\\,y_1+p t_1),\\;(x_1+q t_2,\\,y_1+p t_2)\n\\]\nwith \\(|t_1|,|t_2|\\le n\\) and \\(t_1\\neq t_2\\).\n\nNow impose the condition \\(x+y\\le n+1\\). For the first point we have\n\\(x_1+y_1\\le n+1\\). For the second point,\n\\[\n(x_1+q t_1)+(y_1+p t_1)= (x_1+y_1)+(p+q)t_1\\le n+1 .\n\\]\nSimilarly for the third point,\n\\((x_1+y_1)+(p+q)t_2\\le n+1\\).\n\nIf \\(p+q\\neq 0\\) then the inequalities force both \\(t_1\\) and \\(t_2\\) to have the same sign as \\(-(x_1+y_1-n-1)\\), and their absolute values are bounded by\n\\[\n|t_i|\\le \\frac{n+1-(x_1+y_1)}{|p+q|}\\le \\frac{n}{|p+q|}\\le n .\n\\]\nBut because \\(t_1\\neq t_2\\) and both are integers, we would obtain at least three distinct values of \\(x+y\\) among the three points, contradicting the fact that all three lie in the same arithmetic progression with difference \\(p+q\\) and that the interval \\([1,n+1]\\) contains at most two such values when \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\). Hence \\(|p+q|=1\\).\n\nIf \\(|p+q|=1\\) then the only possibilities for \\((p,q)\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) are\n\\((p,q)=(0,1),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,-1),\\,( -1,1)\\), which correspond respectively to horizontal, vertical and slope \\(-1\\) directions \u2013 all non\u2011sunny. This contradicts the assumption that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.\n\nTherefore a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S_n\\); it contains at most two. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Non\u2011sunny lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA vertical line has equation \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nPoints of \\(S_n\\) on it satisfy \\(c+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such integers \\(b\\).  \nThe same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \nFor a line of slope \\(-1\\) we have the equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); the admissible integer solutions are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\), again at most \\(n\\) points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; by Lemma\u202f2 they together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le 2k+(n-k)n = n^{2}-kn+2k .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) from (1) and simplify:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le n^{2}-kn+2k\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines**.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Sunny lines* (there are \\(k\\) of them):\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (i-1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k)\\;}\n\\]\nThe slope \\(i\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\nFor each \\(i\\) the line \\(\\ell_i\\) meets the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) in exactly the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;i^{2}+i-1),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\;i^{2}+2i),\n\\]\nbecause substituting \\(x=i\\) (resp. \\(x=i+1\\)) into the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\) yields the displayed \\(y\\)-coordinates, and the inequalities\n\\[\ni+(i^{2}+i-1)=i^{2}+2i-1\\le n+1,\\qquad\n(i+1)+(i^{2}+2i)=i^{2}+3i+1\\le n+1\n\\]\nhold for all \\(i\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines* (there are \\(n-k\\) of them):\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;v_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n)\\;}\n\\]\nThese are vertical, hence non\u2011sunny, and distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\ge k+1\\).*  \nThen the vertical line \\(v_a\\) belongs to the family and contains \\((a,b)\\) by definition.\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\le k\\).*  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-k-1=n-k .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(b\\) is at most \\(n-k\\).  \nObserve that for each \\(i\\le k\\) the two points \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) are precisely the points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(i\\) **and** whose second coordinate is congruent to \\(i-1\\) modulo \\(i\\):\n\\[\nP_i:\\; b\\equiv i-1\\pmod{i},\\qquad Q_i:\\; b\\equiv 2i\\pmod{i}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(0\\le b\\le n-k\\) and \\(i\\le k\\), the only possibilities for the residue class of \\(b\\) modulo \\(i\\) are exactly the two residues appearing in \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) must coincide with either \\(P_a\\) or \\(Q_a\\). In either sub\u2011case the point lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the family\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\cup\\{v_{k+1},\\dots ,v_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct, and it covers the whole set \\(S_n\\). Hence a configuration exists for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\) is necessary (Lemma\u202f1, Lemma\u202f2, counting argument) and sufficient (explicit construction). Therefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."

