   \\[\n   i+i\\le 2i\\le 2(n-2)\\le n+1,\\qquad\n   (i+1)+(i+2)=2i+3\\le 2(n-2)+3=n+1,\n   \\]\n   so both points belong to \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines* (\\(n-k\\) of them):\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;v_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n)\\;}\n   \\]\n   These are vertical, hence non‑sunny, and distinct.\n\n4. **Verification of coverage** –  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\ge k+1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(v_a\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\le k\\)*, then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\) to be either \\(a\\) or \\(a+2\\):\n   indeed, the only points of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\) that lie on a line of\n   slope \\(2\\) are \\(P_a\\) and \\(Q_a\\) defined above, and the inequality\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) excludes any other integer \\(b\\) because any other point with\n   \\(x=a\\) would have to satisfy \\(b\\equiv a\\pmod{2}\\) and \\(b\\ge a+4\\), which would give\n   \\(a+b\\ge 2a+4>n+1\\) (recall \\(a\\le k\\le n-2\\)). Hence \\((a,b)\\) equals either\n   \\(P_a\\) or \\(Q_a\\) and therefore belongs to the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\n   Consequently every element of \\(S_n\\) is covered by at least one line of the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\cup\\{v_{k+1},\\dots ,v_n\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion** –  \n   The inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\) is necessary (Lemma 1 + Lemma 2) and sufficient\n   (explicit construction). Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf a line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) (i.e. it is sunny), then it contains at most  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+1\\le n-1\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in the form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+c ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\; q>0,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,\\pm\\infty,-1\\}\\); thus \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).\n\nIf \\((x,y)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line, then  \n\n\\[\nyq=px+qc .\n\\]\n\nHence the integer \\(x\\) determines \\(y\\) uniquely, and two distinct lattice points on the line differ by a vector \\((\\Delta x,\\Delta y)=(q t, p t)\\) for some non‑zero integer \\(t\\).  \nConsequently the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta (x+y)=\\Delta x+\\Delta y = (p+q)t .\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is not of slope \\(-1\\), we have \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\); therefore the values of \\(x+y\\) taken by points of the line form an arithmetic progression with step \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) inside an interval of length \\(n-1\\).  \nHence the progression can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Big\\rfloor+1\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+1 = M\n\\]\n\nterms. Each term corresponds to at most one lattice point of \\(S_n\\), so the line contains at most \\(M\\) points of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in the points\n\\((c,b)\\) where \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such integers \\(b\\).  \nThe same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); the admissible integer\nsolutions are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\), again at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(M\\) points, and by Lemma 2 each of the\nremaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le kM+(n-k)n .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) from (1) and the explicit value \\(M=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le k\\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\bigr)+(n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nA straightforward rearrangement gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  Define the following \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Sunny lines* (there are \\(k\\) of them):\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell_i:\\; y = 2x - i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k)\\;}\n\\]\nThe slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\nFor each \\(i\\) the line \\(\\ell_i\\) meets the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) in exactly the two\nlattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,i+2).\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\ny=2i-i=i\\quad\\text{when }x=i,\\qquad\ny=2(i+1)-i=i+2\\quad\\text{when }x=i+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\le k\\le n-2\\),\n\n\\[\nP_i:\\; i+i=2i\\le 2(n-2)\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\\[\nQ_i:\\; (i+1)+(i+2)=2i+3\\le 2(n-2)+3=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence both points belong to \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny lines* (there are \\(n-k\\) of them):\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;v_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n)\\;}\n\\]\nThese are vertical, thus non‑sunny, and distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\ge k+1\\).*  \nThen the vertical line \\(v_a\\) belongs to the family and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\le k\\).*  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\le k\\le n-2\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(k+1)\\le n-k .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\) are of the form \\((a,b)\\) where\n\\(b\\) is an integer between \\(a\\) and \\(n+1-a\\).  \nIf \\(b\\neq a\\) and \\(b\\neq a+2\\), then \\(b\\ge a+4\\) (the parity of \\(b-a\\) must be even,\notherwise the line of slope \\(2\\) through \\((a,b)\\) would have non‑integer intercept).\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\na+b\\ge a+(a+4)=2a+4\\ge 2(k)+4>n+1,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nThus the only possibilities are \\(b=a\\) or \\(b=a+2\\), i.e. \\((a,b)=P_a\\) or \\((a,b)=Q_a\\).\nBoth points lie on the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\nHence every element of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\cup\\{v_{k+1},\\dots ,v_n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\) is necessary (Lemma 1 + Lemma 2) and sufficient (the explicit\nfamily \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)).  Therefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n∎"
