ints of the line form an arithmetic progression with step \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) inside an interval of length \\(n-1\\).  \nHence the progression can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Big\\rfloor+1\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+1 = M\n\\]\n\nterms. Each term corresponds to at most one lattice point of \\(S_n\\), so the line contains at most \\(M\\) points of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in the points\n\\((c,b)\\) where \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such integers \\(b\\).  \nThe same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); the admissible integer\nsolutions are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\), again at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(M\\) points, and by Lemma 2 each of the\nremaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le kM+(n-k)n .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) from (1) and the explicit value \\(M=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le k\\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\bigr)+(n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nA straightforward rearrangement gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  Define the following \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Sunny lines* (there are \\(k\\) of them):\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell_i:\\; y = 2x - i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k)\\;}\n\\]\nThe slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\nFor each \\(i\\) the line \\(\\ell_i\\) meets the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) in exactly the two\nlattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,i+2).\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\ny=2i-i=i\\quad\\text{when }x=i,\\qquad\ny=2(i+1)-i=i+2\\quad\\text{when }x=i+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\le k\\le n-2\\),\n\n\\[\nP_i:\\; i+i=2i\\le 2(n-2)\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\\[\nQ_i:\\; (i+1)+(i+2)=2i+3\\le 2(n-2)+3=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence both points belong to \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny lines* (there are \\(n-k\\) of them):\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;v_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n)\\;}\n\\]\nThese are vertical, thus non‑sunny, and distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\ge k+1\\).*  \nThen the vertical line \\(v_a\\) belongs to the family and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\le k\\).*  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\le k\\le n-2\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(k+1)\\le n-k .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\) are of the form \\((a,b)\\) where\n\\(b\\) is an integer between \\(a\\) and \\(n+1-a\\).  \nIf \\(b\\neq a\\) and \\(b\\neq a+2\\), then \\(b\\ge a+4\\) (the parity of \\(b-a\\) must be even,\notherwise the line of slope \\(2\\) through \\((a,b)\\) would have non‑integer intercept).\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\na+b\\ge a+(a+4)=2a+4\\ge 2(k)+4>n+1,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nThus the only possibilities are \\(b=a\\) or \\(b=a+2\\), i.e. \\((a,b)=P_a\\) or \\((a,b)=Q_a\\).\nBoth points lie on the sunny line