            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(S_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf a line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) (i.e. it is sunny), then it contains at most  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line as  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+c ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\; q>0,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,\\pm\\infty,-1\\}\\); hence \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).\n\nIf \\((x,y)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line, then  \n\n\\[\nyq=px+qc .\n\\]\n\nThus two distinct lattice points on the line differ by a vector \\((\\Delta x,\\Delta y)=(qt,pt)\\) for some non‑zero integer \\(t\\).  \nConsequently the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta (x+y)=\\Delta x+\\Delta y=(p+q)t .\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is not of slope \\(-1\\), we have \\(p+q\\neq0\\). Moreover \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\): if \\(|p+q|=1\\) then \\(|p|,|q|\\le1\\) and the reduced fraction \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) would be \\(0,\\pm1\\) or \\(\\pm\\infty\\), all excluded. Hence  \n\n\\[\n|p+q|\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\); therefore the possible values of \\(x+y\\) taken by points of the line form an arithmetic progression with step at least \\(2\\) inside an interval of length \\(n-1\\).  \nThe progression can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+1=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor=M\n\\]\n\nterms, and each term corresponds to at most one lattice point of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Non‑sunny lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) where \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such integers \\(b\\).  \nA horizontal line is analogous.  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); the admissible integer solutions are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\), again at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover every point of \\(S_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(M\\) points, and by Lemma 2 each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines contributes at most \\(n\\) points. Hence\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le kM+(n-k)n .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) from (1) and \\(M=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).  \nA short case distinction on the parity of \\(n\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le k\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+(n-k)n\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **\\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible** for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Sunny lines* (there are \\(k\\) of them):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell_i:\\; y = 2x - i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k)\\;}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the intersection of \\(\\ell_i\\) with the integer lattice inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) consists of exactly the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,i+2),\n\\]\n\nbecause substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=i\\) and substituting \\(x=i+1\\) gives \\(y=i+2\\).\n\nSince \\(i\\le k\\le n-2\\),\n\n\\[\nP_i:\\; i+i=2i\\le 2(n-2)\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\\[\nQ_i:\\; (i+1)+(i+2)=2i+3\\le 2(n-2)+3=n+1,\n\\]\n\nso both points belong to \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny lines* (there are \\(n-k\\) of them):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;v_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n)\\;}\n\\]\n\nThese are vertical, hence non‑sunny, and distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification of coverage\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\ge k+1\\).*  \nThen the vertical line \\(v_a\\) belongs to the family and clearly contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\le k\\).*  \nFrom the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and the bound \\(a\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(k+1)\\le n-k .\n\\]\n\nAll integer points of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\) satisfy \\(a\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nIf \\(b\\neq a\\) and \\(b\\neq a+2\\), then \\(b\\ge a+4\\) (the difference \\(b-a\\) must be even because points \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a+1,b+1)\\) would lie on a line of slope \\(1\\), which is forbidden for sunny lines).  \nBut \\(a+(a+4)=2a+4\\ge 2(k)+4>n+1\\) (since \\(k\\le n-2\\)), contradicting \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nHence the only possibilities are \\(b=a\\) or \\(b=a+2\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) equals either \\(P_a\\) or