\\(k\\le n-2\\); Lemmas\u202fB and\u202fC show that this condition is also sufficient. Therefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope \\(\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(c\\) define the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_c=\\{(a,c)\\mid a\\ge 1\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid b\\ge 1\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D_c=\\{(a,c-a)\\mid a\\ge 1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf a line contains two distinct points of \\(\\mathcal H_c\\) it is horizontal, i.e. has slope \\(0\\); similarly two points of \\(\\mathcal V_c\\) force slope \\(\\infty\\) and two points of \\(\\mathcal D_c\\) force slope \\(-1\\). Consequently:\n\n> **Lemma\u202f1.** A sunny line meets each of the sets \\(\\mathcal H_c,\\mathcal V_c,\\mathcal D_c\\) in **at most one** point.\n\nNow consider the points of \\(T_n\\) lying on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\):\n\\[\n(1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1).\n\\]\nThey are \\(n\\) distinct points. By Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line can contain at most one of them, so covering all of them requires either\n* \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, or\n* at least one non\u2011sunny line (the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)).\n\nAnalogously the points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) force the use of at least one vertical (non\u2011sunny) line unless we already used the horizontal one. Hence **any** family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines. Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\