point and the point obtained by moving one unit in the direction of the slope while staying inside the triangle), \\(\\sigma_j\\) covers at least two points of \\(U_{j-1}\\).\n\n*Deletion of redundant non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nAny non\u2011sunny line that becomes completely covered by the already existing lines can be removed.  \nA non\u2011sunny line can become redundant only if all its points are now covered; because each newly added sunny line contributes at least two newly covered points, at most two non\u2011sunny lines can become redundant after the insertion of a single sunny line.\n\nThus after step \\(j\\) we can delete at most two non\u2011sunny lines, keeping the total number of lines \\(\\le n\\).\n\nSince we start with at most \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines and we perform at most \\(k\\le n-2\\) insertions, after the \\(k\\)-th step we still have at most \\(n\\) lines. If we have fewer than \\(n\\) lines, we simply keep some of the original non\u2011sunny lines that have not become redundant; this does not affect the covering property.\n\nThe final family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) therefore consists of\n\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\), and\n* enough non\u2011sunny lines to make the total cardinality exactly \\(n\\),\n\nand it covers every point of \\(T_n\\). Consequently a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom (1) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nSections\u202f2 and\u202f3 show that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines can be constructed.\n\nHence the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
