al number of distinct directions is at most \\(1+3=4\\).  \nEach direction can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\) (a line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) meets the triangle in at most \\(n\\) lattice points).  \nHence the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\(4n\\).  \nSince \\(|S_{n}|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>4n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 7\\), a configuration with a single sunny line is impossible for \\(n\\ge 7\\).  \nA direct inspection of the remaining small values \\(n=3,4,5,6\\) also shows impossibility (the required covering would force at least two sunny lines).  \nThus **\\(k=1\\) can never occur**.\n\n*Step 4 – Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the following \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Horizontal lines }y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y_{h},\\\\\n&\\text{Vertical lines }x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x_{v},\\\\\n&\\text{Diagonal lines }x+y=c_{1},\\,x+y=c_{2},\\dots ,x+y=c_{d},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere the integers \\(h,v,d\\ge 0\\) satisfy \\(h+v+d=n\\) and are chosen so that every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines.  \nA concrete choice is\n\\[\nh=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Big\\rfloor,\\qquad \nv=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{3}\\Big\\rfloor,\\qquad \nd=n-h-v .\n\\]\nOne checks directly that for each \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) either \\(a\\le h\\) (so the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)), or \\(b\\le v\\) (so it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)), or else \\(a+b\\le d+1\\) (so it lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\)).  \nThus all points are covered and all lines are non‑sunny, giving \\(k=0\\).\n\n*Step 5 – Construction for \\(k\\ge 2\\).*  \nStart from the construction of Step 4 (which uses only non‑sunny lines).  \nReplace any two of the non‑sunny lines by two sunny lines that together cover exactly the same set of points.  \nFor example, replace the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=2\\) by the sunny lines\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:\\; y=\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{1}{3},\\