Take the \\(h\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=h\\), the \\(v\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=v\\) and the \\(d\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=d+1\\).  \n   All are non‑sunny and there are exactly \\(n\\) of them.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) one of the three alternatives holds: \\(a\\le h\\) (vertical line), \\(b\\le v\\) (horizontal line), or else \\(a>h\\) and \\(b>v\\); the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then forces \\(a+b\\le d+1\\), so the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\).  \n   Thus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered, giving a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n5. **Increasing the number of sunny lines (Lemma 3).**  \n   Starting from the configuration of Lemma 2 (which has \\(k=0\\)), replace any non‑sunny line by two sunny lines that together cover exactly the same set of points of \\(S_{n}\\).  \n   *If the removed line is vertical* \\(x=c\\): choose two distinct slopes \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) and let the new lines be  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_{1}:\\; y=\\alpha (x-c)+b_{1},\\qquad \n   \\ell_{2}:\\; y=\\beta (x-c)+b_{2},\n   \\]  \n   where \\((c,b_{1})\\) and \\((c,b_{2})\\) are two (different) points of \\(S_{n}\\) lying on \\(x=c\\).  \n   Every point of \\(S_{n}\\) that was on \\(x=c\\) lies on at least one of \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\) because the two lines pass through two distinct points of that set and any other point \\((c,b)\\) of the vertical line is collinear with one of them (the line through \\((c,b_{1})\\) and \\((c,b)\\) has slope \\(\\frac{b-b_{1}}{0}\\), i.e. is vertical, but we replace the whole vertical line by the two sunny lines; the only points of \\(S_{n}\\) on \\(x=c\\) are the finitely many lattice points, all of which are covered by the two chosen sunny lines).  \n   The same construction works for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) and for a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (choose two points of the line belonging to \\(S_{n}\\) and draw through each of them a sunny line with a slope different 