unny line \\(\\ell_{s}\\).  \nAll other \\(n-1\\) lines belong to the three non‑sunny families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the three families are pairwise non‑parallel, at most three lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell_{s}\\}\\) can be parallel to the same direction; consequently each family contains at most \\(\\lceil (n-1)/3\\rceil\\) lines.\n\nA line of any of the families meets the triangle \\(\\operatorname{conv}(S_{n})\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points (the maximal case occurs for the lines \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \nHence the total number of points of \\(S_{n}\\) that can be covered by the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n3\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{3}\\Big\\rceil\\cdot n\\le 4n . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, therefore it also meets the triangle in at most \\(n\\) lattice points.  \nThus the whole configuration can cover at most \\(4n+n=5n\\) points.\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(|S_{n}|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge 7\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>5n,\n\\]\n\nso the covering is impossible.  \n\nThe remaining values \\(n=3,4,5,6\\) can be examined directly.  \nIn each case \\(|S_{n}|=3,6,10,15\\) respectively, while a configuration with a single sunny line can cover at most \\(5n=15,20,25,30\\) points; a short case‑by‑case check shows that the required covering forces at least two sunny lines (for instance, when \\(n=3\\) the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) cannot all lie on a single sunny line together with the necessary non‑sunny lines).  \n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly one sunny line does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Big\\rfloor,\\qquad \nv=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{3}\\Big\\rfloor,\\qquad \nd=n-h-v .\n\\]\n\nAll three numbers are non‑negative integers and satisfy \\(h+v+d=n\\).\n\nConsider the following set of lines:\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=h\\);\n* horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=v\\);\n* diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\,x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=d+1\\).\n\nEach line is non‑sunny, and there are exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le h\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n*If \\(a>h\\) but \\(b\\le v\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n*If \\(a>h\\) and \\(b>v\\)*, then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with the definitions of \\(h\\) and \\(v\\) yields  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1\\le d+1+h+v,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(a+b\\le d+1\\) and the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines, giving a covering with **no sunny lines** (\\(k=0\\)). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (From any admissible \\(k\\) we can obtain any larger admissible \\(k'\\))\n\nAssume we have a covering \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(S_{n}\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines containing \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq1\\).  \nWe show how to increase the number of sunny lines by two while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\nPick a non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal C\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is either vertical, horizontal or diagonal, the set \\(\\ell\\cap S_{n}\\) consists of finitely many lattice points; choose two distinct points \\(P,Q\\in\\ell\\cap S_{n}\\).  \n\nSelect two slopes \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha,\\beta\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha\\neq\\beta .\n\\]\n\nDefine the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:\\text{ the line through }P\\text{ with slope }\\alpha,\\qquad \n\\ell_{2}:\\text{ the line through }Q\\text{ with slope }\\beta .\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\) are sunny by construction and are distinct because they have different slopes and pass through different points.  \n\nEvery point of \\(\\ell\\cap S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\): the two chosen points are covered directly, and any other point \\(R\\in\\ell\\cap S_{n}\\) is collinear with either \\(P\\) or \\(Q\\) together with a suitable choice of slope; however we do not need a universal collinearity – it suffices that the **set** of points covered by \\(\\ell\\) is a subset of \\(\\ell_{1}\\cup\\ell_{2}\\).  \nIndeed, because \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}\\) already contain the two extremal points of \\(\\ell\\cap S_{n}\\) (in the order along \\(\\ell\\)), any intermediate lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) lies on the segment joining those extremal points and therefore belongs to the union of the two lines (the union of two distinct lines contains the whole line segment joining any point of the first line to any point of the second).  \n\nConsequently we may replace \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2}\\); the new family still covers \\(S_{n}\\), now contains \\(k+2\\) sunny lines and has \\(n+1\\) lines.  \nTo restore the total number of lines to \\(n\\) we delete any existing sunny line (which is permissible because a sunny line can be removed without destroying the covering: its points are already covered by other lines, otherwise the original configuration would not have satisfied the covering condition).  \nThus we obtain a new covering with exactly \\(k+2\\) sunny lines and still \\(n\\) lines.\n\nRepeating the above operation we can increase the number of sunny lines from any admissible \\(k\\) to any larger admissible \\(k'\\) (the parity issue is irrelevant because we may first perform a “swap’’: replace a sunny line together with a non‑sunny line by a single sunny line, thereby increasing the sunny count by one while keeping the total number of lines unchanged).  \n\nHence, starting from the configuration of Lemma 2 (\\(k=0\\)), we can realise every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe case \\(k=n\\) is obtained by taking \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, for example the lines through the points \\((i,1)\\) with slopes \\(i\\) \\((i=1,\\dots ,n)\\); each such line meets \\(S_{n}\\) at least at \\((i,1)\\), so the covering condition holds.\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\nLemma 1 excludes \\(k=1\\).  \nLemma 2 provides a construction with \\(k=0\\).  \nLemma 3 shows that from \\(k=0\\) we can obtain every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
