 \\]\n\n   Take the \\(h\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\) and the \\(v\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\).  \n   If \\(n\\) is odd, replace the last vertical line \\(x=v\\) by the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (which is non\u2011sunny).  \n\n   *Verification.*  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) either \\(b\\le h\\) (then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(y=b\\)) or \\(b>h\\). In the latter case we have \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le v\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(x=a\\) (or on the diagonal when the replacement was made). Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n4. **Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   We start from the configuration of step\u202f3 (all non\u2011sunny lines) and replace some of them by sunny lines while preserving the covering property.\n\n   *Choice of slopes.*  \n   For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) pick a distinct integer slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   These slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so the corresponding lines are sunny.\n\n   *Construction of the sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(j\\) we define a line \\(\\sigma_j\\) of slope \\(m_j\\) that passes through the lattice point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_j=(j,\\,n+1-2j).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(j\\ge 1\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\), we have \\(n+1-2j\\ge 1\\) for all \\(j\\le k\\le n-2\\); hence \\(P_j\\in T_n\\).  \n   The line \\(\\sigma_j\\) has equation  \n\n   \\[\n   y-(n+1-2j)=m_j\\,(x-j).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(m_j\\) is an integer, (2) shows that whenever \\((x,y)\\) is a lattice point on \\(\\sigma_j\\) the difference \\(x-j\\) is an integer, and then \\(y\\) is also an integer. In particular the point  \n\n   \\[\n   Q_j=(j+1,\\;n+1-2j+m_j)\n   \\]\n\n   lies on \\(\\sigma_j\\). Because \\(m_j\\le k+1\\le n-1\\) and \\(j\\le k\\le n-2\\),\n\n   \\[\n   Q_j\\;:\\; x=j+1\\ge 1,\\qquad \n   y=n+1-2j+m_j\\le n+1-2j+(k+1)\\le n+1-j\\le n+1-(j+1),\n   \\]