which would already require \\(n\\) lines and would leave no line for the remaining points of \\(T_n\\). Hence at least one horizontal (non‑sunny) line is necessary.  \n\n   The same argument applied to the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\)) shows that at least one vertical (non‑sunny) line is also necessary.  \n   Because the horizontal and vertical lines are distinct, any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains **at least two** non‑sunny lines, whence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A completely non‑sunny configuration (\\(k=0\\)).**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   h=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\qquad v=n-h .\n   \\]\n\n   Take the \\(h\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\) and the \\(v\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\).  \n   If \\(n\\) is odd, replace the last vertical line \\(x=v\\) by the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (which is also non‑sunny).  \n\n   *Verification.*  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) either \\(b\\le h\\) (then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)) or \\(b>h\\). In the latter case we have \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le v\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (or on the diagonal when the replacement was made). Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and all \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n4. **Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   The idea is to start from the configuration of step 3 (all non‑sunny) and to **replace** some of its horizontal (or vertical) lines by sunny lines.  \n   Because of Lemma 1 a sunny line can meet a given horizontal line in at most one point, we must make sure that the points we lose from the removed horizontal line are covered by the new sunny line. This can be done by letting each sunny line pass through **two** points of \\(T_n\\) that originally belonged to the removed horizontal line; then the removed line becomes redundant.\n\n   We treat the two possible ranges of \\(k\\) separately.\n\n   ---\n   **Case A: \\(1\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).**  \n\n   *Base non‑sunny family.*  \n   Use the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) together with the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   This gives \\(n+2\\) lines; we will later delete exactly two of them, leaving \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   P_j=(j,\\;n+1-2j),\\qquad Q_j=(j+1,\\;n+1-2j+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(j\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) we have \\(n+1-2j\\ge 1\\); also \\(j+1\\le k+1\\le n\\) and \\(n+1-2j+1\\le n\\). Hence both \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\) belong to \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Let \\(\\sigma_j\\) be the line of slope \\(2\\) passing through \\(P_j\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_j:\\; y-(n+1-2j)=2\\,(x-j). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(x=j+1\\) into (2) gives \\(y=n+1-2j+2=n+1-2j+1\\), i.e. \\(Q_j\\) also lies on \\(\\sigma_j\\).  \n   The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(\\sigma_j\\) is sunny.\n\n   *Deletion of redundant horizontal lines.*  \n   The horizontal line \\(y=j\\) (which belongs to the original family) contains the point \\(P_j\\) and no other point of the set \\(\\{P_{j'}\\mid j'\\neq j\\}\\). After adding \\(\\sigma_j\\) the point \\(P_j\\) is already covered, and the point \\(Q_j\\) (which lies on the horizontal line \\(y=j+1\\)) is also covered. Consequently the horizontal lines \\(y=j\\) and \\(y=j+1\\) become unnecessary **simultaneously**.  \n   Performing this replacement for each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) removes exactly the \\(2k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=2k .\n   \\]\n\n   *Resulting family.*  \n   After the deletions we keep:\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\);\n   * the remaining horizontal lines \\(y=2k+1,\\dots ,y=n\\) (there are \\(n-2k\\) of them);\n   * the vertical line \\(x=1\\);\n   * the diagonal