"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nThat is, there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   is the right\u2011angled triangular lattice that must be covered.  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **A basic observation (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   A sunny line meets each of the three families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_c=\\{(a,c)\\mid a\\ge 1\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal V_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid b\\ge 1\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal D_c=\\{(a,c-a)\\mid a\\ge 1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   in **at most one** point; otherwise the line would be respectively horizontal,\n   vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n3. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   The points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) all lie on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   If no horizontal line were used, each of these \\(n\\) points would have to be\n   covered by a different sunny line (Lemma\u202f1), which would already consume all\n   \\(n\\) lines and leave no line for the remaining points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence at least one horizontal (non\u2011sunny) line is necessary.  \n   The same argument applied to the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (points\n   \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\)) shows that at least one vertical (non\u2011sunny)\n   line is also necessary.  \n   Because the horizontal and the vertical lines are distinct, any admissible\n   family of \\(n\\) lines contains at least two non\u2011sunny lines, whence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Existence of a completely non\u2011sunny configuration (\\(k=0\\)).**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   h=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\qquad v=n-h .\n   \\]\n\n   Take the \\(h\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\) and the \\(v\\) vertical\n   lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\).  \n   If \\(n\\) is odd, replace the last vertical line \\(x=v\\) by the diagonal\n   line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (which is also non\u2011sunny).  \n\n   *Verification.*  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) either \\(b\\le h\\) (then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the\n   horizontal line \\(y=b\\)) or \\(b>h\\). In the latter case we have\n   \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le v\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (or on\n   the diagonal when the replacement was made). Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) is\n   covered and all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n5. **Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   The idea is to start from the configuration of step\u202f4 and to replace some\n   of its horizontal (or vertical) lines by sunny lines.  \n   Because of Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line can intersect a given horizontal line in at\n   most one point; therefore we let each sunny line pass through **two**\n   points of the triangle that originally belonged to the removed horizontal\n   line, making that line redundant.\n\n   We treat two ranges of \\(k\\) separately.\n\n   ---\n   **Case\u202fA: \\(1\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).**  \n\n   *Base family.*  \n   Keep the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2k\\) and the vertical line\n   \\(x=1\\); also keep the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   At this stage we have \\((n-2k)+2\\) lines.\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   A_j=(j,\\;n+1-2j),\\qquad B_j=(j+1,\\;n-1-2j).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(j\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) we have\n   \\(n+1-2j\\ge 3\\) and \\(n-1-2j\\ge 1\\); moreover\n   \\(A_j,B_j\\in T_n\\) (their coordinates are positive and their sums are\n   \\(n+1-j\\) and \\(n-j\\), respectively, both \\(\\le n+1\\)).  \n\n   Let \\(\\ell_j\\) be the line through \\(A_j\\) and \\(B_j\\).  The slope of\n   \\(\\ell_j\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-1-2j)-(n+1-2j)}{(j+1)-j}= -2,\n   \\]\n\n   which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\n\n   *Deletion of horizontal lines.*  \n   The horizontal line \\(y=n+1-2j\\) (which belongs to the original family) contains\n   the point \\(A_j\\) and no other point of the set \\(\\{A_{j'}\\mid j'\\neq j\\}\\).\n   After adding \\(\\ell_j\\) the point \\(A_j\\) is already covered, and the point\n   \\(B_j\\) (lying on the horizontal line \\(y=n-1-2j\\)) is also covered.\n   Consequently the two horizontal lines\n   \\(y=n+1-2j\\) and \\(y=n-1-2j\\) become unnecessary **simultaneously**.\n   Performing this replacement for each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) removes exactly the\n   \\(2k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=n+1-2,\\;y=n-1-2,\\dots ,y=n+1-2k .\n   \\]\n\n   *Resulting family.*  \n   After the deletions we keep  \n\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\);\n   * the remaining horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2k\\) (there are \\(n-2k\\) of them);\n   * the vertical line \\(x=1\\);\n   * the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-2k)+1+1=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   \u2013 If a point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n-2k\\) it lies on the horizontal line\n   \\(y=b\\).  \n   \u2013 If \\(b>n-2k\\) then \\(b\\) equals either \\(n+1-2j\\) or \\(n-1-2j\\) for some\n   \\(j\\le k\\); in the first sub\u2011case the point is \\(A_j\\) and belongs to\n   \\(\\ell_j\\), in the second sub\u2011case it is \\(B_j\\) and also belongs to\n   \\(\\ell_j\\).  \n   \u2013 Points with \\(a=1\\) are covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   \u2013 Points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are covered by the diagonal line.\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines appear.\n\n   ---\n   **Case\u202fB: \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor < k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   Apply the construction of Case\u202fA after swapping the roles of the \\(x\\)- and\n   \\(y\\)-axes.  \n   Concretely, start from the non\u2011sunny family consisting of the vertical lines\n   \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2(k-(\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor))\\), the horizontal line\n   \\(y=1\\) and the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   A'_j=(\\,n+1-2j,\\;j\\,),\\qquad B'_j=(\\,n-1-2j,\\;j+1\\,),\n   \\]\n\n   and let \\(\\tau_j\\) be the line through them; its slope is \\(+\\tfrac12\\), which\n   is sunny.  Deleting the two vertical lines that contain \\(A'_j\\) and \\(B'_j\\)\n   for each \\(j\\) reduces the total number of lines to \\(n\\).  The verification\n   that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered is symmetric to that of Case\u202fA.\n\n   Consequently a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every\n   integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 yields the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Steps\u202f4,\u202f5\u202fCase\u202fA and\u202f5\u202fCase\u202fB show that this condition is also sufficient.\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]"
