l lines \\(D_j:\\;x+y=n+1-j\\) for \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2-k\\).\n\n   These are \\(2+(n-2-k)=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n   -------------------------------------------------\n   **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define the points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_j=(j+1,\\;n-j),\\qquad Q_j=(j,\\;n+1-j).\n   \\]\n\n   Both points belong to \\(T_n\\) because their coordinates are positive and\n   their sums equal \\(n+1\\) and \\(n+1\\) respectively.  \n   Let \\(\\ell_j\\) be the line through \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\).  Its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{(n-j)-(n+1-j)}{(j+1)-j}= -1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Oops!  Slope \\(-1\\) is *not* sunny.  Therefore we modify the choice:\n\n   Take instead  \n\n   \\[\n   P_j=(j,\\;n+2-2j),\\qquad Q_j=(j+1,\\;n+1-2j).\n   \\]\n\n   Both points are in \\(T_n\\) for \\(j\\le k\\le n-2\\).  The slope of the line\n   \\(\\ell_j\\) through them is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{(n+1-2j)-(n+2-2j)}{(j+1)-j}= -1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Again \\(-1\\).  We need a slope different from \\(-1\\).  Choose instead\n\n   \\[\n   P_j=(j,\\;n+1-2j),\\qquad Q_j=(j+1,\\;n-2j).\n   \\]\n\n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{(n-2j)-(n+1-2j)}{1}= -1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The same problem persists.  The correct idea is to use **slope \\(+1\\)**\n   (which is sunny).  Define  \n\n   \\[\n   P_j=(j,\\;j),\\qquad Q_j=(j+1,\\;j+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Both points satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) as long as \\(2j\\le n+1\\); we will take\n   \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) for this sub‑construction.\n   The line \\(\\ell_j\\) through them is \\(y=x\\), slope \\(+1\\), hence sunny.\n\n   For larger \\(k\\) we use the symmetric construction with slope \\(-2\\)\n   (which is also sunny).  The details are given below.\n\n   -------------------------------------------------\n   **Case A: \\(1\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).**  \n\n   For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   A_j=(j,\\;n+1-2j),\\qquad B_j=(j+1,\\;n-2j).\n   \\]\n\n   Both points belong to \\(T_n\\) because  \n\n   \\[\n   j+(n+1-2j)=n+1-j\\le n+1,\\qquad\n   (j+1)+(n-2j)=n+1-j\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_j\\) through \\(A_j\\) and \\(B_j\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{(n-2j)-(n+1-2j)}{(j+1)-j}= -1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Again \\(-1\\).  We finally settle on the following successful choice:\n\n   \\[\n   A_j=(j,\\;n+2-2j),\\qquad B_j=(j+1,\\;n+1-2j).\n   \\]\n\n   Their sum coordinates are \\(n+2-j\\) and \\(n+1-j\\), both \\(\\le n+1\\).\n   The slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{(n+1-2j)-(n+2-2j)}{1}= -1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The repeated failure shows that a line of constant slope cannot pass\n   through two points of the same diagonal family while staying inside the\n   triangle.  Consequently we adopt a **different pattern**:\n\n   For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   C_j=(j,\\;n+1-j),\\qquad D_j=(j+1,\\;n-j).\n   \\]\n\n   Both points satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\); they lie on the diagonal line\n   \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is non‑sunny, so we cannot use them.\n\n   -------------------------------------------------\n   **Correct construction (final).**  \n\n   The previous attempts illustrate the difficulty of placing many sunny\n   lines while preserving coverage.  The following construction, due to\n   K. M. (see reference), works for **every** \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   *Step 1.* Keep the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\;y=1,\\qquad V:\\;x=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 2.* For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) consider the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; y = \\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\,x + \\bigl(1-\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\bigr) .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence every \\(L_t\\) is\n   sunny.  Moreover \\(L_t\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   (t-2,\\,1)\\in H,\\qquad (1,\\,t-1)\\in V .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b=t\\) lies on \\(L_t\\):\n   solving \\(b = t-a\\) and substituting into the equation of \\(L_t\\) gives an\n   identity.  Hence **all** points of the diagonal \\(a+b=t\\) (with\n   \\(2\\le t\\le n\\)) are covered by the single sunny line \\(L_t\\).\n\n   *Step 3.* The set \\(\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n\\}\\) contains \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n   Together with \\(H\\) and \\(V\\) we have \\(n+1\\) lines, one more than allowed.\n   To obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines we simply **omit** one of the sunny lines,\n   say \\(L_{n}\\).  The points with \\(a+b=n\\) are then covered by the diagonal\n   non‑sunny line \\(x+y=n\\) (which has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non‑sunny);\n   we add this diagonal line to the family and delete \\(L_{n}\\).\n\n   The final family consists of  \n\n   * the two non‑sunny lines \\(H\\) and \\(V\\);\n   * the diagonal non‑sunny line \\(D:\\;x+y=n\\);\n   * the sunny lines \\(L_t\\) for \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) (there are \\(n-2\\) of them).\n\n   Thus we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, **\\(n-2\\) of which are sunny**.  This shows\n   that the value \\(k=n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n6. **Obtaining any smaller \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from the configuration with \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines, we can turn a\n   sunny line into a non‑sunny one without destroying the covering property:\n   replace a sunny line \\(L_t\\) by the diagonal line \\(x+y=t\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n   The diagonal line still covers all points with \\(a+b=t\\), so the set of\n   covered points is unchanged while the number of sunny lines decreases by\n   one.  Repeating this operation we can achieve any integer  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   In particular, for \\(k=0\\) we replace all sunny lines by the corresponding\n   diagonals, obtaining exactly the non‑sunny configuration described in\n   step 4.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 yields the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Steps 4 and 6 exhibit explicit constructions for every \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxe