"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 (intersection bound).**  \n   A sunny line meets each of the three families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H_c=\\{(x,c)\\mid x\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal V_c=\\{(c,y)\\mid y\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal D_c=\\{(x,c-x)\\mid x\\in\\mathbb R\\}\n   \\]\n\n   in at most one point.  \n   *Proof.*  If a sunny line contained two points of a horizontal line\n   \\(\\mathcal H_c\\) it would be horizontal, contradicting the definition of\n   sunny; the same argument works for the vertical and the diagonal families.\n\n3. **Necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   The points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) all lie on the horizontal line\n   \\(y=1\\).  If we used no horizontal line, each of those \\(n\\) points would\n   have to be covered by a different sunny line (Lemma\u202f1), exhausting all\n   \\(n\\) lines and leaving no line for the remaining points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence at least one horizontal (non\u2011sunny) line is required.  \n   By symmetry, at least one vertical (non\u2011sunny) line is also required.\n   Because the horizontal and the vertical lines are distinct, any admissible\n   family of \\(n\\) lines contains at least two non\u2011sunny lines, whence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **A convenient family of non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\) the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s:\\;x+y=s\n   \\]\n\n   has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is non\u2011sunny.  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal N=\\{y=1\\}\\cup\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{D_s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies either \\(b=1\\) (hence lies on\n   \\(y=1\\)), or \\(a=1\\) (hence lies on \\(x=1\\)), or \\(a+b=s\\) for some\n   \\(3\\le s\\le n\\) (hence lies on \\(D_s\\)).  Thus \\(\\mathcal N\\) covers all\n   points of \\(T_n\\) and provides a configuration with **\\(k=0\\)** sunny\n   lines.\n\n5. **Turning a diagonal into a sunny line.**  \n   Fix an integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\).  Choose any integer\n   \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le s-1\\) and consider the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{s,t}:\\; y = \\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\,x + \\Bigl(1-\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\dfrac{t-1}{t-2}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence\n   \\(L_{s,t}\\) is sunny.  Moreover \\(L_{s,t}\\) passes through the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{s,t}=(t-2,1),\\qquad Q_{s,t}=(1,t-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(x=t-2\\) into (2) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=1\\) gives\n   \\(y=t-1\\).  Consequently the whole set of lattice points with sum\n   \\(a+b=s\\) is covered by the *union* of the following two lines:\n\n   * the diagonal \\(D_s\\) (non\u2011sunny), and  \n   * the sunny line \\(L_{s,t}\\) (which contains the two points\n     \\((t-2,1)\\) and \\((1,t-1)\\) that belong to the horizontal and vertical\n     lines already present).\n\n   In particular, if we replace the diagonal \\(D_s\\) by the sunny line\n   \\(L_{s,t}\\) **and keep the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the vertical line\n   \\(x=1\\)**, every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered:\n   * points with \\(a+b=s\\) are now covered by \\(L_{s,t}\\);\n   * points with \\(a+b\\neq s\\) are covered exactly as before.\n\n6. **Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).