ometry of sunny lines\n\nIf \\(L\\) is sunny, its equation can be written uniquely as  \n\n\\[\ny=mx+b,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nor, equivalently, as  \n\n\\[\nx+my=c,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nTwo distinct sunny lines have distinct slopes, hence they intersect in exactly one point.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nI=\\{L_i\\cap L_j\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le n,\\;L_i,L_j\\text{ sunny}\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of all intersection points of pairs of sunny lines.  \nBecause the slopes of sunny lines are all different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\), each point of \\(I\\) has **both coordinates non‑zero** and its two coordinates are **not equal in magnitude**; consequently no point of \\(I\\) lies on any of the three forbidden directions \\(\\ell_x,\\ell_y,\\ell_d\\).\n\nIf there are \\(k\\) sunny lines, then  \n\n\\[\n|I|=\\binom{k}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points that can be covered by non‑sunny lines\n\nA line parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\) (i.e. horizontal) has equation \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\neq0\\).  \nSince all points of \\(T\\) have positive integer \\(y\\)-coordinates, a horizontal line can contain **at most one** point of \\(T\\).  \nSimilarly, a vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(c\\neq0\\)) can contain at most one point of \\(T\\), and a line parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\) (equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\neq0\\)) can contain at most one point of \\(T\\) because the points of \\(T\\) have distinct values of \\(a+b\\).\n\nHence **each non‑sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(T\\)**.  \nSince there are at most three non‑sunny lines (one of each forbidden direction), the total number of points of \\(T\\) that can be covered by non‑sunny lines is at most three.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Counting covered points\n\nAll points of \\(T\\) must belong either to \\(I\\) (intersection of two sunny lines) or to a non‑sunny line.  \nTherefore, using the observations of