grightarrow 4n\\le 8\n\\Longrightarrow n\\le2,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the hypothesis \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Realising every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now construct, for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1  Choice of slopes  \n\nPick \\(k\\) distinct real numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_{1},m_{2},\\dots ,m_{k}\\qquad\\text{with }m_{i}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA convenient choice is \\(m_{i}=i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\); all these slopes are different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\) we shall later define a line  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\;y=m_{i}x+b_{i}.\n\\]\n\nThese will be the sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.2  Assigning points to intersections  \n\nThe number of unordered pairs of sunny lines is \\(\\binom{k}{2}\\).  \nBecause of (4),\n\n\\[\n\\binom{k}{2}\\le\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}- (n-k) .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus we can select a subset  \n\n\\[\nT_{0}\\subset T,\\qquad |T_{0}|=\\binom{k}{2},\n\\]\n\nand a complementary subset  \n\n\\[\nT_{1}=T\\setminus T_{0},\\qquad |T_{1}|= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\binom{k}{2}=n-k+(n-k-1).\n\\]\n\n(Only the cardinalities matter; any concrete choice works.)\n\nNow enumerate the pairs \\(\\{i,j\\}\\) with \\(1\\le i<j\\le k\\) and biject them with the points of \\(T_{0}\\).  \nFor a given pair \\(\\{i,j\\}\\) let the associated point be \\((a_{ij},b_{ij})\\).\n\n#### 3.3  Determining the intercepts  \n\nWe determine the constants \\(b_{1},\\dots ,b_{k}\\) inductively so that for every pair \\(\\{i,j\\}\\)\n\n\\[\nL_{i}\\cap L_{j}=(a_{ij},b_{ij}). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nWrite the equation of \\(L_{i}\\) as \\(y=m_{i}x+b_{i}\\).  \nThe intersection of \\(L_{i}\\) and \\(L_{j}\\) is obtained by solving  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}x+b_{i}=m_{j}x+b_{j}\n\\Longrightarrow\nx=\\frac{b_{j}-b_{i}}{m_{i}-m_{j}},\\q