usly uncovered point of \\(T\\).\n\n#### 3.5  Verification  \n\n*All points are covered.*  \nPoints of \\(T_{0}\\) lie at the intersections of the sunny lines by construction.  \nPoints of \\(T_{1}\\) lie on the non‑sunny lines we have added.\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nWe have produced \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Sunny count.*  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{i}\\) are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence non‑sunny.\n\nTherefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration satisfying the two requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nCombining the upper bound (4) with the explicit construction of Section 3, we conclude that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nNo larger value of \\(k\\) is possible, and every value in this range can be attained."
