            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  No configuration with exactly one sunny line  \n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line \\(L\\).  \nAll other \\(n-1\\) lines belong to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nc_{\\max}= \\max\\{c\\mid y=c\\in\\mathcal H\\},\\qquad\nd_{\\max}= \\max\\{c\\mid x=c\\in\\mathcal V\\}.\n\\]\n\n(If a family is empty set the corresponding maximum is taken as \\(0\\).)  \nConsider the point  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(c_{\\max}+1,\\;d_{\\max}+1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(c_{\\max}\\le n\\) and \\(d_{\\max}\\le n\\), we have  \n\n\\[\na+b=c_{\\max}+d_{\\max}+2\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  Their \\(y\\)-coordinates are at most \\(c_{\\max}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) is not on any horizontal line.  \n\n*Vertical lines.*  Their \\(x\\)-coordinates are at most \\(d_{\\max}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) is not on any vertical line.  \n\n*Diagonal lines.*  For any diagonal line \\(x+y=e\\) we have \\(e\\le c_{\\max}+d_{\\max}<a+b\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) is not on any diagonal line.\n\nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) can be covered only by the unique sunny line \\(L\\).  \nA sunny line has a fixed slope \\(\\neq -1\\); therefore the equation of \\(L\\) can be written as \\(y=mx+q\\) with \\(m\\neq -1\\).  \nFor a given sum \\(s=a+b\\) the line \\(L\\) contains at most one point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\) (otherwise two distinct points with the same sum would give the same slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nNow vary \\(a\\) while keeping \\(a+b\\) equal to the constant \\(a+b=c_{\\max}+d_{\\max}+2\\); we obtain infinitely many lattice points of the form \\((a,\\,c_{\\max}+d_{\\max}+2-a)\\) that all belong to \\(S_n\\).  \nOnly one of them can lie on \\(L\\), so at least one point of \\(S_n\\) remains uncovered – a contradiction.\n\nHence a covering family cannot contain exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction with \\(k=0\\)\n\n**Even \\(n\\).**  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{y=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n/2\\}\\cup\\{x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n/2\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines are horizontal or vertical, therefore non‑sunny.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le n/2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \nOtherwise \\(b>n/2\\); from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n/2\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n**Odd \\(n\\).**  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{y=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le (n-1)/2\\}\\cup\\{x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le (n+1)/2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe same argument as above works, because the largest horizontal intercept is \\((n-1)/2\\) and the largest vertical intercept is \\((n+1)/2\\); any point with \\(b\\) larger than \\((n-1)/2\\) must have \\(a\\le (n+1)/2\\).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers \\(S_n\\) and contains no sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for any \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\).\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-x=i .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\) (different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)), so all \\(L_i\\) are sunny and distinct.  \nNote that \\((i,i)\\in L_i\\).\n\n**Non‑sunny lines.**  \n\n*If \\(m\\) is even* set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le m/2\\}\\cup\\{x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le m/2\\}.\n\\]\n\n*If \\(m\\) is odd* set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le (m-1)/2\\}\\cup\\{x=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le (m+1)/2\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal N\\) are horizontal or vertical, hence non‑sunny.  \nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n|\\{L_i\\}|+|\\mathcal N|=k+m=n .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\mathcal N .\n\\]\n\n**Verification that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(S_n\\).**  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\le h\\), where \\(h\\) is the largest horizontal intercept in \\(\\mathcal N\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal N\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b>h\\).*  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1=k+m+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b = k+m+1-b .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a\\le v\\) where \\(v\\) is the largest vertical intercept in \\(\\mathcal N\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal N\\).\n\nOtherwise \\(a>v\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\na=v+t\\qquad (t\\ge 1).\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(a+b\\le k+m+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le k+m+1-(v+t)=k+(m-v)-t+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(v=\\lceil m/2\\rceil\\) and \\(m-v=\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\), the inequality becomes  \n\n\\[\nb\\le k+\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor+1-t .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t\\ge 1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le k+\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(h=\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nh<b\\le k+h .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(b-h\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\).  Observe that  \n\n\\[\nb-a = b-(v+t)= (b-h)-(t- (v-h)) = (b-h)-t .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(v-h\\) equal