\\).  \n\n   * A careful case analysis shows that every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) belongs to at least one of these lines. The analysis distinguishes three mutually exclusive situations:\n     1. \\(b\\le h\\) – the point lies on a horizontal line.\n     2. \\(b>h\\) and \\(a\\le v\\) – the point lies on a vertical line.\n     3. \\(b>h\\) and \\(a>v\\) – then \\(b-a\\) is an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(k-1\\), so the point lies on the sunny line \\(L_{\\,b-a}\\).\n\n   * The inequalities that guarantee case 3 is possible only when \\(k\\ge1\\) are verified, and the bound \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) guarantees that the horizontal and vertical groups are large enough to make cases 1 and 2 exhaustive for all remaining points.\n\n3. **Verification of distinctness and sunny‑line count** – the sunny lines have slope \\(1\\) (hence are sunny), while the horizontal and vertical lines are not sunny. All lines are distinct because they have different slopes or different intercepts.\n\n4. **Conclusion** – the upper bound and the explicit construction match, establishing that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_n\\).  \nSeparate \\(\\mathcal L\\) into the set \\(\\mathcal S\\) of sunny lines and the set \\(\\mathcal N\\) of non‑sunny lines.\n\n*If a line is non‑sunny* it is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nSuch a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (indeed at most \\(n\\) because the coordinates are bounded by \\(n\\)).  \n\n*If a line is sunny* its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently the line meets each of the two families of grid lines \\(\\{x=i\\}_{i\\ge1}\\) and \\(\\{y=j\\}_{j\\ge1}\\) in at most one point.  \nHence a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(S_n\\) (one with a given \\(x\\)‑coordinate and one with a given \\(y\\)‑coordinate).  \n\nLet \\(s=|\\mathcal S|\\) and \\(t=|\\mathcal N|=n-s\\).  \nCounting the points of \\(S_n\\) covered by the family we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S_n|\n   \\le 2s + n t\n   = 2s + n(n-s)\n   = n^2 - (n-2)s .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\n(n-2)s \\le n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}= \\frac{n(n-2)}{2},\n\\qquad\\text{so}\\qquad\ns \\le \\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\) is an integer,  \n\n\\[\ns\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible family contains at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k,\\qquad\nh:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad\nv:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n*Sunny lines* (slope \\(1\\))  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-x=i\\qquad (0\\le i\\le k-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal lines*  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (1\\le j\\le h).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Vertical lines*  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (1\\le j\\le v).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nClearly the lines in (2) are sunny, while those in (3) and (4) are not.  \nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk+h+v = k+m = n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on a line of the family  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\); thus  \n\n\\[\na\\ge1,\\; b\\ge1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 = k+m+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWe consider three mutually exclusive cases.\n\n---\n\n**Case 1.** \\(b\\le h\\).  \n\nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (definition (3)).\n\n---\n\n**Case 2.** \\(b>h\\) and \\(a\\le v\\).  \n\nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (definition (4)).\n\n---\n\n**Case 3.** \\(b>h\\) **and** \\(a>v\\).  \n\nHence  \n\n\\[\na\\ge v+1,\\qquad b\\ge h+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAdding (6) gives  \n\n\\[\na+b\\ge v+h+2 = m+2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nTogether with (5) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nm+2\\le a+b\\le k+m+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\ge1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus Case 3 can occur only when \\(k\\ge1\\), which is consistent with our construction (if \\(k=0\\) the family consists solely of the horizontal and vertical lines and Cases 1–2 already cover all points, as shown below).\n\nFrom (5) and \\(a\\ge v+1\\) we get  \n\n\\[\nb\\le k+m+1-(v+1)=k+h .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause we are in Case 3 we also have \\(b>h\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nh<b\\le k+h .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ni:=b-h .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(i\\) is an integer satisfying  \n\n\\[\n1\\le i\\le k .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nNow compute the difference \\(b-a\\).  \nWrite \\(a=v+t\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\).  \nUsing \\(v=h\\) when \\(m\\) is even and \\(v=h+1\\) when \\(m\\) is odd, we obtain\n\n\\[\nb-a = (h+i)-(v+t)=i-(v-h)-t .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(m\\) is even* (\\(v=h\\)) we have \\(b-a=i-t\\).  \nSince \\(t\\ge1\\) and (5) together with (9) forces \\(t=1\\) (otherwise \\(b\\) would exceed the upper bound \\(k+h\\)), we get  \n\n\\[\nb-a=i-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\) ranges from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\), the quantity \\(i-1\\) ranges from \\(0\\) to \\(k-1\\).  \nHence \\(b-a\\) equals some integer \\(j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le k-1\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_j\\) (definition (2)).\n\n*If \\(m\\) is odd* (\\(v=h+1\\)) we obtain \\(b-a=i-1-t\\).  \nAgain the bound (9) forces \\(t=1\\), giving \\(b-a=i-2\\).  \nSince \\(1\\le i\\le k\\), the value \\(i-2\\) runs through the set \\(\\{-1,0,\\dots ,k-2\\}\\).  \nThe case \\(i-2=-1\\) cannot occur because \\(b>a\\) (recall \\(b>h\\ge a-1\\) in this parity situation); consequently \\(b-a\\) actually belongs to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) again lies on a sunny line \\(L_{\\,b-a}\\).\n\nIn every sub‑case of Case 3 the point is covered by one of the lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Special case \\(k=0\\).**  \nThen \\(m=n\\), \\(h=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), \\(v=\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) and the family consists only of the horizontal and vertical lines (3)–(4).  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) were not covered by any of them we would have \\(a>v\\) and \\(b>h\\); adding these inequalities yields \\(a+b\\ge v+h+2=n+2\\), contradicting (5).  \nHence all points are covered.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) the constructed family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covers the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Distinctness and sunny‑line count  \n\nThe \\(k\\) lines in (2) have slope \\(1\\) and are therefore sunny.  \nThe horizontal lines have slope \\(0\\) and the vertical lines have undefined slope; neither of these slopes is allowed for a sunny line, so they are non‑sunny.  \nAll lines are distinct because:\n\n* the sunny lines have different intercepts (\\(y-x=i\\) with distinct \\(i\\));\n* the horizontal lines have different \\(y\\)-coordinates;\n* the vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-coordinates;\n* a line of slope \\(1\\) cannot coincide with a horizontal or vertical line.\n\nHence the family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* Any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) can contain at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines (inequality (1)).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) there exists an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covers all required points (construction above).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
