### 1. A configuration with no sunny lines (the base family)\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: x=1,\\\\\nL_2 &: y=1,\\\\\nL_3 &: x+y=n+1,\\\\\nL_{i+2}&: x+y=n+2-i\\qquad (i=2,3,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence none is sunny.\n\n**Lemma 1.** Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of (1).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_1\\).  \n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_2\\).  \n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\ b\\ge2\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the integer \\(s=a+b\\) belongs to \\(\\{3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \n\nIf \\(s=n+1\\) we have \\((a,b)\\in L_3\\); if \\(s=n+2-i\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_{i+2}\\). \u220e\n\nThus the family (1) satisfies the covering requirement with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A bound on the number of points a sunny line can contain\n\n**Lemma 2.** A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in the form \\(ax+by=c\\) with integers \\(a,b,c\\) and \\(\\gcd(a,b)=1\\).  \nThe slope is \\(-a/b\\). The line is sunny iff \\(a\\neq0,\\ b\\neq0\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \n\nIf the line contained three distinct points \\((x_i,y_i)\\in T_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\), then subtracting the equations for two pairs would give\n\\(a(x_i-x_j)+b(y_i-y_j)=0\\). Since \\(\\gcd(a,b)=1\\) and both \\(a,b\\neq0\\), the integer vector \\((x_i-x_j,\\;y_i-y_j)\\) would be a non\u2011zero multiple of \\((b,-a)\\). Hence the differences of the coordinates are proportional to \\((b,-a)\\). Because \\(a\\neq b\\), the three points would be collinear with slope either \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting the assumption that the line is sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. The pivot operation\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal F\\) be a non\u2011sunny line. Choose a point \\(P\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) that also lies on another line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) (such a point exists because each line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\) and \\(|T_n|>n\\)).  \n\nSelect a second point \\(Q\\in T_n\\) different from \\(P\\) such that the line \\(PQ\\) is not parallel to any forbidden direction; this is always possible because from \\(P\\) there are at most three forbidden directions, while there are at least \\(|T_n|-1\\ge 5\\) other points to choose from when \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\nReplace \\(\\ell\\) by the sunny line \\(PQ\\). The new family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines and still covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (the points formerly covered only by \\(\\ell\\) are now covered by \\(PQ\\); all other points remain covered).  \n\nThus one non\u2011sunny line can be turned into a sunny line without destroying the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Realising all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nStart from the base family (1) which has exactly two non\u2011sunny lines, namely \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\).  \nApply the pivot operation to each of the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines \\(L_3,\\dots ,L_n\\) one after another. After performing the operation \\(t\\) times we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines. Since \\(t\\) can be any integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\), every \\(k\\) in that interval is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, they could cover at most \\(2n\\) points, but  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n\\]\n\nHence \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Upper bound for \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nSuppose exactly one line, say \\(\\ell\\), is non\u2011sunny.  \nA non\u2011sunny line can be vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); in each case it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the line \\(x=1\\) contains the points \\((1,1),\\dots,(1,n)\\), etc.).  \n\nAll other \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny and by Lemma\u202f2 each of them contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n+2(n-1)=3n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInequality (2) is equivalent to \\(n^2-5n+4\\le0\\), i.e. \\((n-1)(n-4)\\le0\\).  \nThus (2) can hold only for \\(n=3,4\\).  \n\nHence \\(k=n-1\\) is possible only when \\(n=3\\) or \\(n=4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Explicit constructions for the exceptional cases\n\n*Case \\(n=3,\\ k=2\\).*  \nThe three points of \\(T_3\\) are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \nTake  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &: x=1 \\quad(\\text{non\u2011sunny}),\\\\\n\\ell_2 &: y-2x+3=0 \\quad(\\text{sunny, slope }-2),\\\\\n\\ell_3 &: y-x=0 \\quad(\\text{sunny, slope }1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_1\\) covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\); \\(\\ell_2\\) covers \\((2,1),(1,3)\\); \\(\\ell_3\\) covers \\((2,2),(3,1)\\). All six points are covered.\n\n*Case \\(n=4,\\ k=3\\).*  \n\\(T_4\\) consists of ten points.  \nChoose  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &: x=1 \\quad(\\text{non\u2011sunny}),\\\\\n\\ell_2 &: y-3x+4=0 \\quad(\\text{sunny, slope }-3),\\\\\n\\ell_3 &: y-2x+3=0 \\quad(\\text{sunny, slope }-2),\\\\\n\\ell_4 &: y-x=0 \\quad(\\text{sunny, slope }1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell_1\\) covers the three points with \\(x=1\\); the three sunny lines each cover two of the remaining points, giving a complete covering.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8. Conclusion\n\nCombining the constr