ny lines (\\(1\\le k\\le n\\)), we proceed as follows.\n\n   *Select \\(k\\) distinct indices* \\(i_1,\\dots ,i_k\\) from \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n   For each selected index \\(i_j\\) replace the vertical line \\(x=i_j\\) by a sunny line\n   \\[\n   S_{i_j}:\\; y = 2(x-i_j)+1 .\n   \\]\n   (Any line whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\) will do; the concrete choice above is convenient because it passes through the lattice point \\((i_j,1)\\).)\n\n   The new family of lines is\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\bigl\\{S_{i_1},\\dots ,S_{i_k}\\bigr\\}\\cup\n                \\bigl\\{x=i\\mid i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus\\{i_1,\\dots ,i_k\\}\\bigr\\}\n                \\cup\\{y=1\\}.\n   \\]\n   By construction \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n5. **Verification that the covering still holds.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\notin\\{i_1,\\dots ,i_k\\}\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and covers the point.  \n   - If \\(a=i_j\\) for some \\(j\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{i_j}\\) **iff** it satisfies\n     \\[\n     b = 2(a-i_j)+1 = 1 .\n     \\]\n     Hence the only points of the form \\((i_j,b)\\) that are not covered by the sunny line are those with \\(b\\neq 1\\).  \n     Such points are nevertheless covered by the line \\(y=1\\) when \\(b=1\\); otherwise they satisfy \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \n     For \\(b\\ge 2\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a=i_j\\le n-1\\), which forces\n     \\[\n     b\\le n+1-i_j\\le n-1 .\n     \\]\n     Consequently the point \\((i_j,b)\\) also lies on the vertical line \\(x=i_j\\) **before** it was removed; after removal we replace that vertical line by the sunny line \\(S_{i_j}\\), but the point \\((i_j,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) still lies on the sunny line because\n     \\[\n     b = 2(i_j-i_j)+1 = 1 \\quad\\text{is false,}\n     \\]\n     however we can adjust the definition of the sunny line to pass through **all** 