e \\(H:\\;y=1\\), and for each selected index \\(i_j\\) we keep the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_{i_j}:\\; x+y = n+1-i_j .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\bigl\\{S_{i_1},\\dots ,S_{i_k}\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{V_i\\mid i\\notin\\{i_1,\\dots ,i_k\\}\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\{H\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{D_{i_1},\\dots ,D_{i_k}\\bigr\\}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, \\(k\\) diagonal lines, \\(n-1-k\\) vertical lines and one horizontal line, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Verification of the covering\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\notin\\{i_1,\\dots ,i_k\\}\\)*, the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)) covers the point.\n\n*If \\(a=i_j\\) for some \\(j\\)*, then \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i_j\\) by definition of \\(T_n\\).\n\n- When \\(b=1\\) the point \\((i_j,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{i_j}\\) because substituting \\(x=i_j\\) into (4) yields \\(y=1\\).\n\n- When \\(b\\ge 2\\) we have \\(a+b = i_j+b \\le n+1\\). Hence \\(a+b\\) equals the constant on the diagonal line \\(D_{i_j}\\) defined in (5). Consequently \\((i_j,b)\\) lies on \\(D_{i_j}\\), which is a member of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Distinctness of the lines\n\nThe vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, the horizontal line is unique, the diagonal lines have distinct constants \\(n+1-i_j\\), and the sunny lines have distinct slopes \\((n+1-i_j)\\). No two of these equations coincide, so the \\(n\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have constructed a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every point of \\(T_n\\), and  \n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence all integers \\(k\\) in the interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) are admissible. The case \\(k=n\\) is not settled by the present argument. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
