diagonals among the \\(n\\) and use them as the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines.  \n   Each chosen diagonal covers all still\u2011uncovered points on that diagonal, so after adding these \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line.\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n   Hence a configuration exists for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n4. **Lemma 2 (non\u2011existence for \\(k=n-1,n\\)).**  \n   Suppose a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.  \n   Any non\u2011sunny line (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the set of admissible \\(x\\)-coordinates, respectively \\(y\\)-coordinates, or the length of a diagonal).  \n   If \\(s=n-k\\) denotes the number of non\u2011sunny lines, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n s+2k=n(n-k)+2k=n^{2}-(n-2)k. \\tag{\u2217}\n   \\]\n\n   *Case \\(k=n-1\\).*  Formula \\((\u2217)\\) gives at most \\(3n-2\\) points.  \n   Since \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>3n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (the inequality is immediate for \\(n=3,4\\) and holds for larger \\(n\\) because the left\u2011hand side grows quadratically while the right\u2011hand side grows linearly), the required set cannot be covered.  \n\n   *Case \\(k=n\\).*  Then \\((\u2217)\\) yields at most \\(2n\\) points, while \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence this case is also impossible.\n\n   Consequently no configuration can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Steps\u202f3 and\u202f4 together show that the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely the integers  \n\n   \\[\n   0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of points to be covered  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_d=\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=d\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(D_d\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and contains exactly the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(d\\); its cardinality is \\(d-1\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{d=2}^{n+1}(d-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma 1 \u2013 a sunny line meets at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).\n\nIf two of the points share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, \\(\\ell\\) is vertical, slope \\(\\infty\\), contradicting sunniness.  \nIf two share the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, slope \\(0\\), again a contradiction.\n\nThus the three points have pairwise distinct \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-coordinates.  \nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) computed from any two of them is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_j-b_i}{a_j-a_i}\\qquad(i\\neq j).\n\\]\n\nBecause each point satisfies \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), a straightforward algebraic check shows that the equality  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=\\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}\n\\]\n\nforces \\(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)\\) and \\(b_3-b_1=-(a_3-a_1)\\); i.e. the common slope must be \\(-1\\).  \nHence \\(\\ell\\) would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), also forbidden.  \nTherefore a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 3.1  Choosing the sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) pick the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i+1,\\;n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i,\\;n+2-i).\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)+(n+1-i)=n+2\\le n+1\\quad\\text{fails,}\n\\]\n\nso we correct the choice: instead take  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+2-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\;n+1-i).\n\\]\n\nNow  \n\n\\[\ni+(n+2-i)=n+2\\le n+1\\;\\text{?}\n\\]\n\nThis is impossible; we must select points whose sum does not exceed \\(n+1\\).  \nA simpler explicit selection is the following greedy one.\n\nList the diagonals \\(D_3,D_4,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) (the diagonal \\(D_2\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\)).  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) choose a point \\(R_i\\) on \\(D_{i+2}\\) distinct from \\((1,1)\\) (for instance \\(R_i=(1,i+1)\\)).  \nChoose a second point \\(S_i\\) on the next diagonal \\(D_{i+3}\\) (e.g. \\(S_i=(2,i+1)\\)).  \nThe two points lie on different diagonals, hence the line through them cannot have slope \\(-1\\).  \nBecause the two points have different \\(x\\)-coordinates and different \\(y\\)-coordinates, the line cannot be horizontal or vertical.  \nThus the line \\(L_i\\) through \\(R_i\\) and \\(S_i\\) is already sunny; no rotation is needed.\n\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct (they intersect the family of diagonals in distinct pairs) and each contains exactly the two points used to define it.  \nConsequently every diagonal \\(D_{d}\\) with \\(d\\ge3\\) loses **at most one** of its points, while \\(D_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) loses none.\n\n#### 3.2  Remaining uncovered points  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal U=\\mathcal P_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_i\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points not yet covered.  \nFrom the discussion above, each diagonal \\(D_d\\) still contains at least one point of \\(\\mathcal U\\).\n\n#### 3.3  Adding non\u2011sunny (diagonal) lines  \n\nThere are \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).  \nSince at most one point has been removed from each of the \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\), at least two of these diagonals are completely untouched.  \nSelect any \\(n-k\\) distinct diagonals among the \\(n\\) and denote them