Thus a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  Then (2) yields  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|\\le 2n,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nHence \\(k=n\\) is also impossible.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nThe construction of Section\u202f3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realized, and Lemma\u202f2 proves that \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.  \nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
