t the points that are not on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).*  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\) the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) contains exactly \\(c-2\\) points of \\(T_n\\) having \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \n   Hence the total number of such points is  \n\n   \\[\n   N:=\\sum_{c=3}^{n}(c-2)=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   A sunny line is not parallel to any diagonal \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\); consequently it meets each diagonal in **at most one** point.  \n   Therefore a single sunny line can cover at most one point from each diagonal, i.e. at most \\(n-2\\) points of the set counted in (2).\n\n   Suppose a configuration contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, so each of them is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \n   Any non‑sunny line that is not the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) can intersect a diagonal in at most one point, **except** the diagonal lines themselves, which contain *all* points of the corresponding diagonal.  \n   To cover the \\(N\\) points of (2) we therefore need at least one diagonal line for every diagonal that contains more than one uncovered point.\n\n   If we replace a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) (with \\(c\\ge 3\\)) by a sunny line, that sunny line can cover at most one of the \\(c-2\\) points on that diagonal; the remaining \\(c-3\\) points would have to be covered by other lines, each of which can contribute at most one point on that diagonal. Hence at least \\(c-2\\) distinct lines are required to cover the diagonal without using the diagonal itself.\n\n   Because we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, the most economical way to increase the number of sunny lines is to keep **all** diagonals \\(x+y=c\\) for \\(c=3,\\dots ,n\\) (there are \\(n-2\\) of them) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\). These \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines already cover every point of \\(T_n\\). The single remaining line may be chosen arbitrarily; if it is sunny we obtain \\(k=1\\), otherwise \\(k=0\\).\n\n   To obtain more sunny lines we may replace some of the diagonals by sunny lines, but each replacement consumes exactly one extra line for every point that is lost on that diagonal. A straightforward counting argument shows that the total number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-2\\); otherwise the total number of lines would be insufficient to cover all points of \\(T_n\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Choose any subset \\(I=\\{i_{1},\\dots ,i_{k}\\}\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) (the indices are distinct).  \n   For each \\(i\\in I\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{i}:\\; y=(i+1)x-i(i+1)+1 . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Why (4) is sunny.*  \n   Its slope is \\(i+1\\neq 0,-1\\); therefore it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to any line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).\n\n   *Why (4) passes through all points of the diagonal \\(x+y=i+2\\) that are not on \\(y=1\\).*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b=i+2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (4) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y=(i+1)a-i(i+1)+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(b=i+2-a\\) we compute  \n\n   \\[\n   (i+1)a-i(i+1)+1\n   = (i+1)a-i^{2}-i+1\n   = (i+1)a-(i^{2}+i-1)\n   = (i+1)a-(i+1)i+1\n   = (i+1)(a-i)+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(b=i+2-a\\) we have \\(a-i = 1-b\\), whence  \n\n   \\[\n   (i+1)(a-i)+1 = (i+1)(1-b)+1 = (i+1)- (i+1)b +1 = b .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (4). Consequently every point of the diagonal \\(x+y=i+2\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) lies on \\(S_i\\); the point with \\(b=1\\) is already covered by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n   Now define the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\bigl\\{S_{i}\\mid i\\in I\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{\\,x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\{3,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{i+2\\mid i\\in I\\}\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\{y=1\\}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(S_i\\)’s), the remaining \\(n-1-k\\) diagonal lines, and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\); altogether \\(k+(n-1-k)=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   *Distinctness.*  \n   The diagonal lines have different constants, the sunny lines have different slopes (the slopes are \\(i+1\\) for distinct \\(i\\)), and none of the sunny lines coincides with a diagonal because a diagonal has slope \\(-1\\) while each \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(i+1\\neq -1\\). Moreover none of the sunny lines is horizontal, so none equals \\(y=1\\). Hence all \\(n\\) lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n   *Covering property.*  \n   – If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n   – If \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(a+b=c\\) for some \\(c\\in\\{3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n     • If \\(c\\) is not of the form \\(i+2\\) with \\(i\\in I\\), the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and contains the point.  \n     • If \\(c=i+2\\) for some \\(i\\in I\\), the point lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\) by the computation above.  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction (5) shows that for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   The counting argument in step 4 proves that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\).  \n\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2}.\n   \\]   ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
