troduce **another** line to cover the remaining points. Hence each diagonal that is replaced consumes one extra line. Since we have only \\(n\\) lines altogether, we can replace at most \\(n-2\\) diagonals. Therefore\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix a subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).  \n   For each \\(c\\in I\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{c}:\\; y = \\frac{c-2}{c-1}\\,x -\\frac{(c-2)(c-3)}{c-1}+1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Why (4) is sunny.*  \n   Its slope \\(\\dfrac{c-2}{c-1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or undefined (the denominator \\(c-1\\neq0\\) for \\(c\\ge3\\)). Hence the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to any line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).\n\n   *Why (4) passes through all points of the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\).*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\). Then \\(b=c-a\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (4) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{c-2}{c-1}a -\\frac{(c-2)(c-3)}{c-1}+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   A short computation shows that the right‑hand side equals \\(c-a\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\frac{c-2}{c-1}a -\\frac{(c-2)(c-3)}{c-1}+1\n   &=\\frac{(c-2)a-(c-2)(c-3)+(c-1)}{c-1}\\\\\n   &=\\frac{(c-2)(a-c+3)+(c-1)}{c-1}\\\\\n   &=\\frac{(c-2)(a-c+2)+ (c-2)+1}{c-1}\\\\\n   &=\\frac{(c-2)(a-c+2)}{c-1}+ \\frac{c-1}{c-1}\\\\\n   &=a-c+2+1 = c-a .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(y=b\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_c\\). The point of the same diagonal with \\(b=1\\) is already covered by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n   Now define the family of \\(n\\) lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\bigl\\{S_{c}\\mid c\\in I\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{\\,x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\{3,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus I\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\{y=1\\}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   The set \\(\\{S_c\\mid c\\in I\\}\\) contributes \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   The set \\(\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\{3,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus I\\}\\) contributes \\((n-2)-k\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines.  \n   Together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) we have \\(k+(n-2-k)+1=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   *Distinctness.*  \n   All diagonal lines have slope \\(-1\\); all sunny lines have slope \\(\\dfrac{c-2}{c-1}\\neq -1\\) and are pairwise different because the slopes are distinct for distinct \\(c\\). None of the sunny lines is horizontal, so none coincides with \\(y=1\\).\n\n   *Covering property.*  \n   – If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n   – If \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\in\\{3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n     • If \\(c\\notin I\\) the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and contains the point.  \n     • If \\(c\\in I\\) the point lies on the sunny line \\(S_c\\) by the computation above.  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction (5) shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   The counting argument of step 4 proves that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\).  \n\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2}.\n   \\]   ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
